Object.assign((window.PYQ_EXPLAIN = window.PYQ_EXPLAIN || {}), {"ugc-2025-06-p2-1":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Ek ke baad ek, **bina replacement**, aur order matter karta hai (1st, 2nd, 3rd card) → **permutation**: ⁴²P₃ = 42 × 41 × 40 = **68880**.","concept":"### Counting ke 2 basic rules\n- **Sum rule (OR)**: kaam A ya B (dono ek saath nahi) → ways = m + n.\n- **Product rule (AND)**: pehle A phir B → ways = m × n.\n\n### Permutation (order matter karta hai)\n- n alag cheezon me se r ko **line me / order me** lagana: **ⁿPᵣ = n! / (n − r)! = n(n−1)(n−2)…(r terms)**.\n- Saari n cheezein arrange: n!.\n- **Repetition allowed** (with replacement) → nʳ (har position pe n choice).\n- **Identical objects** ho (jaise MISSISSIPPI): n! / (n₁! n₂! …).\n- **Circular arrangement**: (n − 1)! (necklace/garland jisme ulta-seedha same: (n−1)!/2).\n\n### Combination (sirf group, order nahi)\n- **ⁿCᵣ = n! / (r!(n − r)!)** = ⁿPᵣ / r! (kyunki ek group ke r! orders same gine jaate).\n- ⁿCᵣ = ⁿCₙ₋ᵣ, ⁿC₀ = 1, ⁿCᵣ + ⁿCᵣ₋₁ = ⁿ⁺¹Cᵣ (Pascal).\n\n### Kaunsa lagana hai — keywords\n- \"arrange, one after another, in order, positions, president/secretary, password\" → **P**.\n- \"select, choose, committee, group, team, hand of cards\" → **C**.\n- \"with replacement / repetition allowed\" → nʳ.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. **3 cards with replacement**, order matter → 42³ = 74088.\n2. **Kitne alag 3-card hands** (order nahi) → ⁴²C₃ = 68880 / 3! = 11480.\n3. **5 log, 3 kursiyaan** → ⁵P₃ = 60. **Committee of 3 from 5** → ⁵C₃ = 10.\n4. **\"LEVEL\" ke arrangements** → 5! / (2! 2!) = 30.\n5. **6 log round table** → (6 − 1)! = 120.\n6. **4-digit PIN, digits repeat allowed** → 10⁴; repeat nahi → ¹⁰P₄ = 5040.","tips":"- Pehle socho \"order matter karta hai?\" — yahi P vs C decide karta hai.\n- Options me hamesha ek **trap** hota hai: yahan 1722 = 42 × 41 (sirf 2 cards) aur 6880 (zero chhoot gaya).","sol":"1. \"One after the other\", without replacement → order matter, repetition nahi → permutation.\n2. 1st card: 42 tarike, 2nd: 41, 3rd: 40 (product rule).\n3. ⁴²P₃ = 42 × 41 × 40 = 1722 × 40 = **68880**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 68880**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 6.1 (Basics of counting), 6.3 (Permutations and combinations), 6.5 (Generalized permutations)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-2":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Rule: **kisi event/I-O ka intezaar → Waiting**, **sirf CPU chhina gaya (time slice / interrupt) → Ready**. I/O ke baad seedha Ready (A) ✗, time slice ke baad Waiting (C) ✗, interrupt ke baad Waiting (D) ✗. Sirf B possible.","concept":"### Process ke states (5-state model)\n1. **New** — process ban raha hai.\n2. **Ready** — memory me hai, CPU milne ka intezaar (ready queue).\n3. **Running** — CPU pe instructions chal rahe hain.\n4. **Waiting / Blocked** — kisi **event** ka intezaar (I/O complete, child terminate, semaphore signal).\n5. **Terminated** — kaam khatam.\n\n### Transitions (kab kya hota hai)\n- New → Ready: **admitted** (long-term scheduler).\n- Ready → Running: **dispatch** (short-term scheduler / dispatcher).\n- Running → Ready: **preemption** — time quantum khatam (timer interrupt) ya higher-priority process aaya ya koi interrupt aaya.\n- Running → Waiting: process ne **khud** I/O request ki, `wait()` kiya (child ke liye), semaphore pe block hua.\n- Waiting → Ready: wo event ho gaya (I/O complete interrupt).\n- Running → Terminated: `exit()` ya error.\n\n### Kabhi nahi hone wale transitions\n- **Waiting → Running** seedha ✗ (pehle Ready).\n- **Ready → Waiting** ✗ (wait karne ke liye process ko chalna padta hai).\n- **New → Running** ✗.\n\n### Queues\n- **Ready queue** (Ready state ke process), **device/I-O queues** (Waiting ke process, har device ki alag), **job queue**.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Which transition is **not** possible?\" → Waiting→Running, Ready→Waiting.\n2. \"Timer interrupt aane pe running process kahan jaata hai?\" → Ready.\n3. \"Suspended states\" (7-state model): Ready-Suspend, Blocked-Suspend — process swap out (disk pe) hone pe; medium-term scheduler.\n4. \"Kaunsa scheduler Ready → Running karta hai?\" → short-term (CPU) scheduler.\n5. \"Zombie vs orphan\": zombie = child khatam par parent ne `wait()` nahi kiya; orphan = parent pehle khatam.","tips":"- Ek line yaad: **Event ka wait = Waiting, CPU ka wait = Ready.**\n- \"Could not occur\" questions me har statement ko is rule se check karo.","sol":"- A. I/O request ke baad **ready** queue me ✗ — I/O request = event ka wait → Waiting (device queue). → **nahi ho sakta**.\n- B. Child process bana ke uske terminate hone ka wait ✓ — `fork()` + `wait()` → Waiting. → ho sakta hai.\n- C. Time slice khatam → **waiting** queue me ✗ — sirf CPU chhina, event ka wait nahi → Ready. → **nahi ho sakta**.\n- D. Interrupt se hata kar **waiting** queue me ✗ — preemption → Ready. → **nahi ho sakta**.\nAnswer = A, C, D.\n\n**Answer: (D) A, C and D only**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 3.1.2 (Process state diagram), 3.2 (Scheduling queues, queueing diagram).\n- William Stallings — Operating Systems: Internals and Design Principles, Sec 3.2 (Five-state and suspend models)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-3":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Count-to-infinity** = distance vector routing ki problem. **802.16 = WiMAX**, **802.15 = Bluetooth/WPAN**, **AODV** = MANET (mobile ad hoc network) routing protocol.","concept":"### Routing ke 2 main types\n- **Distance Vector (DV)** — har router apni poori distance table sirf **neighbours** ko bhejta hai; Bellman-Ford. Example: **RIP**. Problem: **count-to-infinity** (bad news dheere failti hai).\n- **Link State (LS)** — har router poore network ka map (link costs) **sabko flood** karta hai, phir Dijkstra. Example: **OSPF**, IS-IS. Count-to-infinity nahi.\n- **Path vector** — BGP (inter-domain).\n\n### Count-to-infinity\nLink toot jaaye to A ka neighbour B purani table ke basis pe kehta \"mere through 2 hops\" → A use maan leta → dono ek doosre ki galat cost badhaate rehte (3, 4, 5, … ∞). Fix: **split horizon**, **poison reverse**, **hold-down timers**, max hop (RIP me 16 = infinity).\n\n### IEEE 802 standards\n- 802.3 → Ethernet (wired LAN)\n- 802.5 → Token Ring\n- 802.11 → Wi-Fi (WLAN)\n- **802.15 → WPAN**: 802.15.1 **Bluetooth**, 802.15.4 **Zigbee**\n- **802.16 → WiMAX** (wireless MAN, broadband)\n- 802.1Q → VLAN\n\n### MANET routing protocols\n- **Reactive (on-demand)**: **AODV**, DSR — route tabhi dhundho jab bhejna ho (RREQ flood, RREP).\n- **Proactive (table-driven)**: DSDV, OLSR.\n- **Hybrid**: ZRP.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Split horizon kis problem ko solve karta hai?\" → count-to-infinity (DV).\n2. \"OSPF uses which algorithm?\" → Dijkstra (link state).\n3. \"802.11 / 802.3 / 802.15.4 kya hai?\" → Wi-Fi / Ethernet / Zigbee.\n4. \"DSR vs AODV\" → dono reactive; DSR source routing (poora path packet me), AODV hop-by-hop tables.\n5. \"WiMAX ki range / use\" → MAN, last-mile broadband.","tips":"- Number trick: **11** = WiFi, **15** = \"personal\" (Bluetooth), **16** = \"metro\" (WiMAX).\n- \"Ad hoc / mobile\" word dekho → MANET → AODV/DSR.","sol":"A → II (DV routing), B → III (WiMAX), C → IV (Bluetooth), D → I (MANET).\n\n**Answer: (B) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I**","refs":"- Tanenbaum — Computer Networks, Sec 5.2.4 (Distance vector, count-to-infinity), 5.2.5 (Link state), 4.5–4.6 (802.15 Bluetooth, 802.16).\n- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch. 15–16 (Wireless LANs, Bluetooth, WiMAX), Ch. 20 (Unicast routing).\n- C. Siva Ram Murthy & B.S. Manoj — Ad Hoc Wireless Networks, Ch. 7 (AODV, DSR)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-4":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Little's law**: n = λ × W. Queue me average n = 20 process, arrival rate λ = 10 per second → W = n / λ = 20 / 10 = **2 seconds**.","concept":"### Little's formula\nKisi bhi stable queueing system (steady state) me:\n**n = λ × W**\n- **n** = system/queue me average processes (customers)\n- **λ** = average arrival rate (per unit time)\n- **W** = har process ka average waiting time (system me bitaya time)\nKyu sahi hai: ek process W time tak queue me rehta hai; is dauraan naye λ·W process aa chuke hote hain — wahi average queue length hai. Ye kisi bhi scheduling algorithm aur arrival distribution ke liye valid hai.\n\n### CPU scheduling algorithms evaluate karne ke tarike (Galvin)\n1. **Deterministic modeling** — ek fixed workload pe har algorithm ka exact waiting time (Gantt chart).\n2. **Queueing models** — arrival aur service distributions se formulas (Little's law yahin).\n3. **Simulation** — trace-driven ya random workload.\n4. **Implementation** — asli OS me lagaakar dekhna.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. W = 3 s, λ = 7/s → n = 21 processes.\n2. n = 14, W = 2 s → λ = 7 per second.\n3. \"Little's law kis scheduling pe depend karta hai?\" → kisi pe nahi (general).\n4. Server utilisation ρ = λ / μ (μ = service rate); ρ < 1 hona chahiye warna queue infinite.","tips":"- Units hamesha match: λ per second → W seconds; λ per minute → W minutes.\n- Formula ulta karna aana chahiye: W = n/λ, λ = n/W.","sol":"W = n / λ = 20 / 10 = **2 seconds**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 02 seconds**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 5.8 (Algorithm evaluation; 5.8.2 Queueing models, Little's formula).\n- Leonard Kleinrock — Queueing Systems Vol. 1, Ch. 2 (Little's result)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-5":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Poole & Mackworth: **agent system = agent + environment** (A sahi). **Actuators noisy/unreliable** hote hain (C galat). Stimuli ko percepts me **sensors** badalte hain, actuators nahi (D galat). A sirf option (A) me hai.","concept":"### Agent kya hai\nJo **environment ko perceive kare (sensors)** aur us par **act kare (actuators)**. AI = rational agents banana.\n\n### Poole & Mackworth model\n- **Agent system** = **agent** + **environment**.\n- Agent = **body** + **controller**.\n  - **Body**: **sensors** (environment ke **stimuli → percepts**) aur **actuators** (controller ke **commands → actions** in environment).\n  - **Controller**: body se percepts leta, decide karta, body ko commands deta. (Controller ke paas sirf percepts aur memory hai.)\n- **Sensors aur actuators dono noisy/unreliable** ho sakte — wheel slip, sensor galat reading.\n\n### Russell & Norvig ke agent types (aksar puchhe jaate)\n1. **Simple reflex** — sirf current percept (condition-action rules).\n2. **Model-based reflex** — internal state (world model) rakhta.\n3. **Goal-based** — goal tak pahunchne ke actions plan karta.\n4. **Utility-based** — \"kitna khush/achha\" (utility) maximize karta — happy/unhappy states.\n5. **Learning agent** — learning element, performance element, critic, problem generator.\n\n### Environment properties\nFully vs partially observable, deterministic vs stochastic, episodic vs sequential, static vs dynamic, discrete vs continuous, single vs multi-agent. **PEAS** = Performance, Environment, Actuators, Sensors.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Which agent handles happy/unhappy states?\" → utility-based.\n2. \"Taxi driver ke PEAS\" → P: safe/fast, E: roads, A: steering/brake, S: camera/GPS.\n3. \"Chess with clock\" → fully observable, strategic, sequential, semi-dynamic, discrete.\n4. \"Stimuli → percepts kaun karta?\" → sensors.","tips":"- Elimination: pakka-sahi statement (A) kis option me hai — yahan sirf (A).\n- \"Always reliable / never noisy\" → AI me almost hamesha galat.","sol":"- A ✓ (agent + environment).\n- B: controller percepts leta aur commands bhejta hai; textbook me commands \"body ko\" jaati hain jo environment pe act karti hai — key ise sahi maanti hai.\n- C ✗ (actuators noisy/unreliable ho sakte).\n- D ✗ (stimuli → percepts = sensors ka kaam).\nA sirf option (A) me hai; C, D wale options galat.\n\n**Answer: (A) A, B only**","refs":"- Poole & Mackworth — Artificial Intelligence: Foundations of Computational Agents, Sec 2.1–2.2 (Agents, body and controller).\n- Russell & Norvig — Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, Ch. 2 (Intelligent agents, agent types, PEAS)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-6":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Main memory ke block ko cache ki kis line me rakhna — is process ko **mapping** kehte hain (direct, associative, set-associative).","concept":"### Cache kyu\nCPU fast, main memory slow. Cache = chhoti fast memory jisme recently/frequently used data. **Locality of reference** (temporal + spatial) ki wajah se kaam karta hai.\n\n### Mapping (Morris Mano: \"transformation of data from main memory to cache memory is referred to as a mapping process\")\n1. **Associative mapping**: koi bhi block kisi bhi line me. Address = tag + word. Sabse flexible, par saare tags parallel compare (costly CAM).\n2. **Direct mapping**: block j → line (j mod m). Address = tag | index | word. Sasta, par conflict misses.\n3. **Set-associative (k-way)**: lines ko sets me; block j → set (j mod sets), set ki kisi bhi line me. Beech ka raasta.\n\n### Related terms\n- **Hit ratio** h = hits / total accesses. **Average access time** = h·t_c + (1 − h)·(t_c + t_m) (ya h·t_c + (1−h)·t_m, question ke model pe depend).\n- **Replacement**: LRU, FIFO, Random (direct me zarurat nahi).\n- **Write policies**: write-through, write-back (dirty bit).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. 64 KB cache, 16 B block, 32-bit address, direct → lines = 4096 (12-bit index), offset 4, tag 16.\n2. \"Kaunsi mapping me replacement algorithm nahi chahiye?\" → direct.\n3. \"Associative mapping me kaunsi memory?\" → content addressable memory (CAM).\n4. h = 0.9, t_c = 10 ns, t_m = 100 ns → 0.9·10 + 0.1·(110) = 20 ns (hierarchical model).","tips":"- Mano ki book ki definitions UGC NET me shabdashah aati hain — \"mapping process\" yaad rakho.","sol":"Main memory → cache data ke transformation/placement = **mapping**.\n\n**Answer: (C) Mapping**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 12.6 (Cache memory: associative, direct, set-associative mapping; writing into cache).\n- Hamacher — Computer Organization, Sec 8.6 (Mapping functions)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-7":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Knowledge base banane ke steps (Poole & Mackworth): **domain chuno → atoms chuno → axiomatize (true facts likho) → questions puchho** = C → A → D → B.","concept":"### Knowledge-based system\n- **Knowledge base (KB)**: facts + rules (domain ki knowledge) — declarative.\n- **Inference engine**: KB se naye facts/answers nikaalta (forward/backward chaining).\n\n### KB designer ke 4 steps (Poole & Mackworth, propositional KB)\n1. **Choose a task domain** — kaunsi duniya model karni hai (e.g. ek ghar ki electrical wiring).\n2. **Associate an atom with each proposition** jo represent karna hai (e.g. `light_l1_on`).\n3. **Tell the system** wo clauses jo intended interpretation me true hain = **axiomatize the domain**.\n4. **Ask questions** about the intended interpretation — system jo answer de usse interpret karo.\n\n### Good knowledge representation ki properties (Rich & Knight)\n- **Representational adequacy** — saari zaruri knowledge represent ho sake.\n- **Inferential adequacy** — naye facts derive karne ki ability.\n- **Inferential efficiency** — inference ko efficient direction me le jaana.\n- **Acquisitional efficiency** — naya knowledge aasaani se add ho.\n\n### KR techniques\nLogic (propositional, FOL), semantic networks, frames, scripts, production rules, ontologies.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Not a property of good KR\" → jo upar ki 4 me na ho (e.g. \"presentation adequacy\").\n2. \"Frames kaunsi KR?\" → structured (slots & fillers).\n3. \"Forward vs backward chaining\" → data-driven vs goal-driven.","tips":"- Logical order socho: **World → Symbols → Facts → Query**.","sol":"C (domain) → A (atoms) → D (axiomatize) → B (questions).\n\n**Answer: (A) C → A → D → B**","refs":"- Poole & Mackworth — AI: Foundations of Computational Agents, Sec 5.1–5.2 (Propositions and the knowledge base designer's steps).\n- Rich, Knight & Nair — Artificial Intelligence, Ch. 4 (Knowledge representation issues)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-8":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Outer loop i = 1, 3 (i < 5, step 2). Inner loop j = 1 se **j < i** tak. i = 1 → koi j nahi. i = 3 → sirf j = 1 print. Output **1**.","concept":"### for loop ka execution order\n`for (init; condition; update) body`\n1. init ek baar.\n2. condition check → false ho to loop khatam (body **ek baar bhi nahi** chalti).\n3. body.\n4. update → wapas step 2.\n\n### Nested loop trace karne ka tarika\nTable banao: outer variable ki har value ke liye inner loop ki saari values aur output.\n\n### Common loop traps\n- `for(...);` — semicolon se empty body.\n- `i < n` vs `i <= n` — ek extra iteration.\n- Step 2 se odd/even values hi aati hain.\n- `while(i--)` — pehle check (purani value), phir decrement.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Same code, `j <= i` → i=1: 1; i=3: 1 3 → output \"1 1 3\".\n2. `for(i=0;i<3;i++) for(j=i;j<3;j++) count++;` → 3 + 2 + 1 = 6.\n3. `for(i=1;i<=10;i*=2)` → i = 1, 2, 4, 8 → 4 iterations.","tips":"- Hamesha table banao — options me \"1 1 3\" (`<=` wala trap) aur \"1 3\" diye hote hain.","sol":"- i = 1: j = 1, 1 < 1 false → kuch print nahi.\n- i = 3: j = 1 → print 1; j = 3, 3 < 3 false → ruk.\n- i = 5: 5 < 5 false → outer loop khatam.\nOutput: **1**\n\n**Answer: (A) 1**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 3.5 (Loops — while and for)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-9":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"A = x(y′z)′ = x(y + z′) (De Morgan) = xy + xz′. **Complete SOP** = har term me teeno variables: xy → xyz + xyz′, xz′ → xyz′ + xy′z′ → **xyz + xyz′ + xy′z′**.","concept":"### Boolean algebra ke important laws\n- **De Morgan**: (AB)′ = A′ + B′, (A + B)′ = A′B′.\n- Involution: (A′)′ = A.\n- Complement: A + A′ = 1, A·A′ = 0.\n- Absorption: A + AB = A, A(A + B) = A.\n- Distributive: A(B + C) = AB + AC, A + BC = (A + B)(A + C).\n\n### SOP aur POS\n- **SOP** (sum of products): product terms ka OR, jaise xy + x′z.\n- **POS** (product of sums): sum terms ka AND.\n- **Canonical / complete SOP** = har product term me **saare variables** → **minterms** ka sum (Σm). Missing variable v ko (v + v′) se multiply karke expand karo.\n- **Canonical POS** = **maxterms** ka product (ΠM).\n- n variables → 2ⁿ minterms. Σm(list) ka complement = Σm(baaki).\n\n### Minterm number\nx y z = 1 1 0 → m6. Yahan xyz = m7, xyz′ = m6, xy′z′ = m4 → A = Σm(4, 6, 7).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Canonical POS of same A → ΠM(0, 1, 2, 3, 5).\n2. \"Complement of A\" → Σm(0, 1, 2, 3, 5).\n3. Simplify Σm(4, 6, 7) → x(y + z′) = xy + xz′ (K-map se).\n4. \"Number of minterms in canonical form of x + y (3 variables)\" → 6.","tips":"- **Shortcut check**: x = 0 pe A = 0 → canonical form me koi x′ term nahi ho sakta. Isse (A), (B), (C) turant kat jaate.","sol":"1. De Morgan: (y′z)′ = y + z′ → A = xy + xz′.\n2. xy = xy(z + z′) = xyz + xyz′.\n3. xz′ = xz′(y + y′) = xyz′ + xy′z′.\n4. Duplicates hatao: **xyz + xyz′ + xy′z′** = Σm(4, 6, 7).\n\n**Answer: (D) xyz + xyz′ + xy′z′**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Digital Design, Sec 2.4 (Basic theorems), 2.6 (Canonical and standard forms)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-10":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Reliability** = stated conditions me performance maintain karna. **Efficiency** = performance vs resources. **Maintainability** = badlaav ka effort. **Portability** = dusre environment me le jaana.","concept":"### ISO/IEC 9126 quality model (6 characteristics)\n1. **Functionality** — required functions (suitability, accuracy, interoperability, security).\n2. **Reliability** — stated conditions me stated time tak performance maintain karna (maturity, fault tolerance, recoverability).\n3. **Usability** — samajhne, seekhne, use karne ka effort.\n4. **Efficiency** — performance level aur use hue **resources** (time, memory) ka relationship.\n5. **Maintainability** — modifications ka effort (analysability, changeability, stability, testability).\n6. **Portability** — ek environment (organisation/hardware/software) se doosre me **transfer** (adaptability, installability, replaceability).\nYaad: **FRUEMP**.\n\n### McCall's quality factors (11, 3 groups)\n- **Product operation**: Correctness, Reliability, Efficiency, Integrity, Usability.\n- **Product revision**: Maintainability, Flexibility, Testability.\n- **Product transition**: Portability, Reusability, Interoperability.\n\n### FURPS (HP): Functionality, Usability, Reliability, Performance, Supportability.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Which is NOT a McCall factor?\" → Functionality (ye ISO/FURPS me hai).\n2. \"Product transition factors\" → portability, reusability, interoperability.\n3. \"Fault tolerance kis characteristic ka sub-part?\" → reliability.","tips":"- Naam se meaning: **porta**ble = le ja sakte, **maintain** = badalna/sudharna, **effic**ient = kam resources.","sol":"A → II (reliability), B → III (efficiency), C → IV (maintainability), D → I (portability).\n\n**Answer: (B) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I**","refs":"- ISO/IEC 9126-1 (Software engineering — Product quality, Part 1: Quality model).\n- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 19.2 (McCall's factors, ISO 9126)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-11":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Morris Mano: **Address space** = virtual addresses ka set, **Memory space** = physical addresses ka set. **Cache** → associative mapping. **Segmented program** → logical address (segment + offset).","concept":"### Virtual memory (Morris Mano ki language)\n- Programmer ko lagta hai bahut badi memory hai; asal me chhoti main memory + disk.\n- **Address space** = program jo addresses generate karta hai = **virtual addresses** ka set (N).\n- **Memory space** = main memory ki locations = **physical addresses** ka set (M), usually M < N.\n- **Address mapping**: virtual → physical, memory **page table** se (pages ↔ blocks/frames).\n\n### Paging vs Segmentation\n- **Paging**: fixed-size pages; user ko dikhai nahi deta; internal fragmentation.\n- **Segmentation**: variable-size logical units (code, data, stack, function); programmer ko dikhta; CPU **logical address = (segment, offset)**; external fragmentation. Segmented-paging dono milata.\n\n### Cache organisations\n- **Associative mapping** → content addressable memory (CAM), sabse fast/flexible.\n- Direct, set-associative.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Address space 2²⁰ words, memory 2¹⁵, page 1K → pages = 1024, blocks = 32, page table = 1024 entries.\n2. \"Logical address kaun generate karta?\" → CPU. \"Physical address kaun dekhta?\" → memory unit.\n3. \"Segmentation se kaunsi fragmentation?\" → external. Paging → internal.\n4. \"TLB kya hai?\" → page table entries ka associative cache.","tips":"- Pair yaad: **Address space ↔ Virtual**, **Memory space ↔ Physical**.","sol":"A → IV (virtual), B → III (physical), C → I (associative mapping), D → II (logical address).\n\n**Answer: (A) A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 12.5 (Virtual memory: address space and memory space, page table), 12.6 (Cache), 12.7 (Memory management: segmentation).\n- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Ch. 9 (Main memory: paging, segmentation)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-12":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Digital signature** → authenticity + integrity. **Hash function** → fixed-size digest. **AES** → symmetric. **RSA** → asymmetric.","concept":"### Security goals (CIA + more)\nConfidentiality (sirf sahi log padhein), Integrity (badla na jaaye), Availability, **Authentication** (kaun bhej raha), **Non-repudiation** (bhejne wala mukar na sake).\n\n### Symmetric key cryptography\n- Ek hi **shared secret key** se encrypt aur decrypt. Fast.\n- Block ciphers: **DES** (64-bit block, 56-bit key), **3DES** (168-bit key), **AES** (128-bit block, 128/192/256-bit key), Blowfish, Twofish, IDEA.\n- Stream ciphers: **RC4**.\n- Problem: key distribution (n users → n(n−1)/2 keys).\n\n### Asymmetric (public key)\n- Har user ka **public key** (sabko) + **private key** (secret). Ek se encrypt, doosre se decrypt.\n- **RSA** (factorisation), Diffie-Hellman (key exchange), ElGamal, ECC.\n- Confidentiality: receiver ki public key se encrypt. Signature: sender ki private key se sign.\n\n### Hash function\n- Kisi bhi length ka input → **fixed-length digest** (MD5 128-bit, SHA-1 160-bit, SHA-256 256-bit).\n- One-way, collision-resistant. Integrity check ke liye.\n\n### Digital signature\nSender message ka **hash** apni **private key** se encrypt (sign) karta → receiver sender ki **public key** se verify → **authenticity + integrity + non-repudiation**. (Confidentiality nahi deta.)\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Digital signature kaunsi key se banta?\" → sender ki private key.\n2. \"DES ka key size\" → 56 bits (64 with parity). \"AES block size\" → 128 bits.\n3. \"Which provides non-repudiation?\" → digital signature (MAC nahi).\n4. \"MAC vs signature\" → MAC shared secret key (symmetric), signature private key.","tips":"- **A**ES = **a**lways same key (symmetric). **R**SA = public/private.","sol":"A → II (authenticity + integrity), B → III (fixed digest), C → IV (AES symmetric), D → I (RSA asymmetric).\n\n**Answer: (B) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I**","refs":"- William Stallings — Cryptography and Network Security, Ch. 3–6 (DES, AES), Ch. 9 (RSA), Ch. 11 (Hash functions), Ch. 13 (Digital signatures).\n- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch. 31–32."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-13":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"C me **a[i] ≡ *(a + i)**. Teen subscripts → teen baar: ar[m][n][0] = *( *( *(ar + m) + n) + 0 ).","concept":"### Array aur pointer ka rishta (C)\n- Array ka naam (most contexts me) uske **pehle element ka address** ban jaata hai.\n- **a[i] ≡ *(a + i) ≡ *(i + a) ≡ i[a]** (ye last wala bhi legal hai!).\n- `a + i` element-size ke hisaab se badhta (int a[]: a + 1 = 4 bytes aage).\n\n### Multi-dimensional arrays\n`int ar[X][Y][Z];` row-major me store.\n- `ar[m]` = `*(ar + m)` → m-th 2D block (type: int[Y][Z])\n- `ar[m][n]` = `*(*(ar + m) + n)` → n-th row (int[Z])\n- `ar[m][n][k]` = `*(*(*(ar + m) + n) + k)` → int value\n- Address formula (row-major): base + ((m·Y + n)·Z + k) × size.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. `int a[3][4]` me `a[1][2]` = `*(*(a+1)+2)`; address = base + (1·4 + 2)·4 = base + 24.\n2. `*(a+1)` aur `a[1]` same? Haan.\n3. `int *p = &a[0][0]; p[5]` = a[1][1] (4 columns).\n4. `sizeof(a)` = 48, `sizeof(a[0])` = 16 (int 4 bytes).","tips":"- Ek subscript = ek `*( … + index)`; bahar se andar likho.\n- Options me `o` (letter) vs `0` (zero) aur missing `*` ka trap dekho.","sol":"ar[m][n][0] = *(*(*(ar + m) + n) + 0). (A) me m, n same level pe add (galat); (B), (C) me `o` aur bahar ka `*` nahi.\n\n**Answer: (D) *(*(*(ar+m)+n)+0)**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — Sec 5.3 (Pointers and arrays), 5.7 (Multi-dimensional arrays), 5.9 (Pointers vs multi-dimensional arrays).\n- Yashavant Kanetkar — Let Us C / Understanding Pointers in C (3-D arrays with pointers)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-14":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"\"Only context free\" = CFG hai **par regular grammar nahi**. Regular grammar = har production **ek hi side** linear (sab left-linear ya sab right-linear). A context-sensitive (LHS `aS`). B, E poori left-linear (regular). **C** (S → AS) aur **D** (left + right mix) → only CF.","concept":"### Chomsky hierarchy (grammar ke types)\n- **Type 0** — unrestricted: α → β (α me kam se kam ek non-terminal) — Turing machine — recursively enumerable.\n- **Type 1** — context-sensitive: αAβ → αγβ, |γ| ≥ 1 (|LHS| ≤ |RHS|) — linear bounded automaton — CSL.\n- **Type 2** — context-free: **A → γ** (LHS pe sirf ek non-terminal) — pushdown automaton — CFL.\n- **Type 3** — regular: **A → aB | a** (right-linear) ya **A → Ba | a** (left-linear) — finite automaton — regular language.\nHar Type 3 ⊂ Type 2 ⊂ Type 1 ⊂ Type 0.\n\n### Regular grammar ke rules\n- RHS me **zyada se zyada ek non-terminal**.\n- Wo non-terminal **hamesha right end** (right-linear) ya **hamesha left end** (left-linear) — **ek grammar me mix nahi**. Mix (S → aA, S → Ab) wali grammar ko \"linear grammar\" kehte hain, regular grammar nahi (language phir bhi regular ho sakti hai).\n\n### Is question ke grammars (image se)\n- A: S → Ab, **aS → aA**, A → a → LHS pe 2 symbols → context-sensitive.\n- B: S → Ab, A → Sa, A → a → sab **left-linear** → regular.\n- C: S → **AS**, S → aA, A → a → RHS me 2 non-terminals → CFG only.\n- D: S → Ab (left), S → aA (right) → mixed → CFG only.\n- E: S → Sb, A → Aa, A → ε → left-linear → regular.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"S → aSb | ε kaunsa type?\" → Type 2 (aⁿbⁿ, regular nahi).\n2. \"Type 1 ka machine?\" → LBA. \"Type 0?\" → TM.\n3. \"Which grammar is ε-free?\" → Type 1 (S → ε ko chhod ke).\n4. \"Linear grammar always regular?\" → nahi (S → aSb linear hai par aⁿbⁿ).","tips":"- Regular check: RHS me ek NT, aur hamesha **ek hi kinare**.","sol":"CFG but not regular grammar = C, D.\n\n**Answer: (B) C, D only**","refs":"- Peter Linz — An Introduction to Formal Languages and Automata, Sec 3.3 (Right/left-linear regular grammars), 5.1 (CFG), 11.4 (Chomsky hierarchy).\n- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Ch. 5 (CFGs), Ch. 9 (Turing machines and the hierarchy of languages)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-15":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"UDP header = 8 bytes = 4 fields × 2 bytes: **source port | destination port | length | checksum**. Pehle 4 hex digits = source port = 0xCB84 = **52100**.","concept":"### UDP (User Datagram Protocol)\n- **Connectionless**, unreliable, no flow/congestion control, no retransmission, no ordering. Fast, kam overhead.\n- Use: DNS (53), DHCP (67/68), SNMP (161), TFTP (69), RIP (520), VoIP, streaming.\n\n### UDP header — 8 bytes\n| Source port (16) | Destination port (16) | Total length (16) | Checksum (16) |\n- Total length = header (8) + data.\n- Checksum IPv4 me optional (0 = not used).\n\n### TCP header (compare)\n20–60 bytes: source/dest port, sequence no., ack no., header length, flags (URG, ACK, PSH, RST, SYN, FIN), window, checksum, urgent pointer, options.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai (same hex CB84000D001C001C)\n1. Destination port = 0x000D = **13** (daytime service).\n2. Total length = 0x001C = **28 bytes** → data = 28 − 8 = **20 bytes**.\n3. Client se server ya ulta? Source port 52100 (ephemeral, > 1023) → **client → server**.\n4. \"UDP header ka minimum size\" → 8 bytes. \"TCP\" → 20 bytes.","tips":"- Hex → decimal: C=12, B=11. 0xCB84 = 12·4096 + 11·256 + 8·16 + 4.\n- Ye exact question Forouzan ki book ka example hai.","sol":"0xCB84 = 12×4096 + 11×256 + 8×16 + 4 = 49152 + 2816 + 128 + 4 = **52100**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 52100**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch. 24 (UDP user datagram; Example with CB84000D001C001C).\n- Kurose & Ross — Sec 3.3 (UDP segment structure)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-16":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"GA termination (Sivanandam & Deepa): **Worst individual** → poori population minimum standard pe. **Best individual** → fast convergence, kam se kam ek best. **Sum of fitness** → lagbhag sab ek fitness range me. **Median fitness** → kam se kam aadhe convergence value se better/equal.","concept":"### Genetic Algorithm (GA) ka basic flow\n1. **Encoding** — solution ko chromosome me (binary, real, permutation, value, tree).\n2. **Initial population** random.\n3. **Fitness evaluation** — har individual ka fitness f(x).\n4. **Selection** (parents) — roulette wheel, tournament, rank, Boltzmann, stochastic universal sampling.\n5. **Crossover** (probability p_c) — single-point, two-point, uniform, PMX/order (permutation).\n6. **Mutation** (probability p_m, chhoti) — bit flip, swap, inversion, scramble, random resetting.\n7. **Replacement** → new generation.\n8. **Termination** check.\n\n### Termination / stopping criteria\n- Maximum generations; elapsed time; no change in best fitness for k generations; stall generations/time.\n- Population-statistics based (Sivanandam & Deepa):\n  - **Best individual**: best member ki fitness ≥ threshold → **faster convergence, kam se kam ek best solution**.\n  - **Worst individual**: worst bhi threshold paar → **poori population minimum standard pe**.\n  - **Sum of fitness**: total fitness ≤ threshold → **lagbhag saare individuals ek particular fitness range me**.\n  - **Median fitness**: median threshold paar → **kam se kam aadhe individuals convergence value se better/equal**.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Which is not a selection method?\" → jo list me na ho (e.g. \"Radial\").\n2. \"Which is not a mutation operator?\" → e.g. \"Difference\".\n3. \"GA steps ka order\" → initialise → evaluate → select → crossover → mutate.\n4. \"Elitism kya hai?\" → best individuals ko seedha next generation me copy karna.","tips":"- Naam se socho: median = \"aadhe\", worst = \"sab minimum standard\", best = \"kam se kam ek\".","sol":"A (worst) → III, B (best) → IV, C (sum) → II, D (median) → I.\n\n**Answer: (A) A→III, B→IV, C→II, D→I**","refs":"- S.N. Sivanandam & S.N. Deepa — Introduction to Genetic Algorithms, Ch. 3 (Operators, Sec 3.13 Stopping conditions).\n- David Goldberg — Genetic Algorithms in Search, Optimization and Machine Learning, Ch. 1–3."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-17":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"\"**Sab** brands own karne wale\" = **division (÷)**. π_eid,cid(Own) ÷ π_cid(Comp) → woh eid jo **har** cid ke saath Own me hain.","concept":"### Relational algebra operators\n- **σ (select)**: rows filter. **π (project)**: columns choose (duplicates hatte).\n- **∪, −, ∩**: union-compatible relations chahiye (same attributes).\n- **× (Cartesian product)**: har row × har row (|R|·|S| rows).\n- **⋈ (join)**: θ-join, equi-join, natural join; outer joins (⟕ ⟖ ⟗).\n- **ρ (rename)**.\n- **÷ (division)**: R(A, B) ÷ S(B) = { a | **har** b ∈ S ke liye (a, b) ∈ R }.\n\n### Division kaise nikaalte hain (basic operators se)\nR ÷ S = π_A(R) − π_A( (π_A(R) × S) − R ).\nMatlab: sab candidate a × har b banao, usme se jo R me nahi (missing pairs) unke a hata do.\n\n### \"For all\" queries → division\n- \"Students who took **all** courses\", \"suppliers who supply **all** parts\", \"employees who own **all** brands\".\n- \"At least one / some\" → join/project (division nahi).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Enrolled(sid, cid) ÷ π_cid(Course) → saare courses lene wale students.\n2. \"Division ko kin operators se express kar sakte?\" → π, ×, − .\n3. |R| = 6, |S| = 2 → |R × S| = 12.\n4. TRC me \"for all\": { t | ∃e ∈ Emp (t.eid = e.eid ∧ ∀c ∈ Comp ∃o ∈ Own (o.eid = e.eid ∧ o.cid = c.cid)) }.","tips":"- Keyword \"**all / every**\" dikhe → division.\n- Divisor ke attributes dividend me hone chahiye: (eid, cid) ÷ (cid) ✓.","sol":"π_eid( π_eid,cid(Own) ÷ π_cid(Comp) ) = eids jo har cid (company/brand) own karte hain. Cartesian product wale options har combination banate hain (galat).\n\n**Answer: (A)**","refs":"- Korth, Silberschatz, Sudarshan — Database System Concepts, Sec 6.1 (Relational algebra, division operation).\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Sec 8.3.4 (DIVISION)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-18":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Existing software ko analyse karke uska **design/hidden information** nikaalna = **Reverse Engineering**.","concept":"### Software maintenance ke types\n- **Corrective** — bugs theek karna.\n- **Adaptive** — naye environment (OS, hardware, laws) ke hisaab se badalna.\n- **Perfective** — naye features / performance improve (user requests).\n- **Preventive** — future problems rokne ke liye restructuring (code refactoring).\n\n### Reverse engineering, re-engineering, forward engineering\n- **Reverse engineering** — existing code (aksar bina docs) analyse karke **higher-level abstraction** (design, data structures, specs) **recover** karna; \"difficult, unknown, hidden information\" nikaalna.\n- **Restructuring** — code/data ko better form me (functionality same).\n- **Forward engineering** — requirements → design → code (normal path), ya recovered design se naya system.\n- **Re-engineering** = reverse engineering + restructuring + forward engineering.\n- **Software re-engineering process model** (Pressman): inventory analysis → document restructuring → reverse engineering → code restructuring → data restructuring → forward engineering.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Modifying software to match changes in environment\" → adaptive maintenance.\n2. \"Restructuring to prevent future problems\" → preventive.\n3. \"Re-engineering me pehla step\" → inventory analysis.","tips":"- Reverse = **peeche** (code → design). Re-engineering = poora naya banana.","sol":"Hidden/unknown design information nikaalna = reverse engineering.\n\n**Answer: (C) Reverse Engineering**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Ch. 36 (Maintenance and re-engineering: reverse engineering, restructuring, forward engineering).\n- Ian Sommerville — Software Engineering, Ch. 9 (Software evolution, legacy system management)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-19":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Layered file system (upar se neeche): **Application programs → Logical file system → File-organization module → Basic file system → I/O control → Devices**.","concept":"### Layered file system (Galvin)\n1. **Application programs** — `open()`, `read()`, `write()` system calls.\n2. **Logical file system** — **metadata** manage: directory structure, **FCB (file control block / inode)**, protection, security.\n3. **File-organization module** — files aur unke **logical blocks → physical blocks** mapping; **free-space manager**.\n4. **Basic file system** — device driver ko generic commands (\"drive 1, cylinder 73, track 2, sector 10\"); buffers aur caches.\n5. **I/O control** — **device drivers** aur **interrupt handlers**.\n6. **Devices** (disk).\nFayda: layering se code reuse; nuksan: har layer ka overhead.\n\n### File allocation methods\n- **Contiguous** — fast sequential + direct access, external fragmentation.\n- **Linked** — no external fragmentation, sirf sequential access, pointer overhead.\n- **Indexed** — direct access, index block overhead (inode me direct + single/double/triple indirect).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"FCB / inode kaunsi layer manage karti?\" → logical file system.\n2. \"Free-space management kaun?\" → file-organization module.\n3. \"Device drivers kis layer me?\" → I/O control.\n4. \"Kaunsi allocation sequential + direct dono support kare bina external fragmentation?\" → indexed.","tips":"- Upar = user ke paas (\"logical\"), neeche = hardware ke paas (\"I/O control\").","sol":"E (application) → A (logical FS) → B (file-organization) → C (basic FS) → D (I/O control).\n\n**Answer: (C) E → A → B → C → D**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 14.1 (File-system structure, layered file system), 14.4 (Allocation methods)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-20":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Software: **wear out nahi hota**, **manufacture nahi, develop/engineer** hota hai, **flexible** hai. \"Always correct\" bilkul galat — software me bugs hote hain.","concept":"### Software ki characteristics (Pressman)\n1. **Developed or engineered, not manufactured** — cost engineering/design me lagti hai, copy banana almost free.\n2. **Doesn't wear out** — hardware ka \"bathtub curve\" (shuru me failures, phir kam, phir ghisne se badhte); software ka curve shuru me bugs ke baad flat hona chahiye, par **changes** se naye bugs → actual curve me spikes → software **deteriorate** hota hai (wear out nahi).\n3. **Custom built** zyadatar (component-based reuse badh raha hai).\n4. **Flexible / malleable** — aasaani se badla ja sakta (isliye change requests bahut).\n5. **Complex, invisible, intangible**.\n\n### Software ke types (application domains)\nSystem software, application software, engineering/scientific, embedded, product-line, web/mobile apps, AI software.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Hardware failure curve ko kya kehte?\" → bathtub curve.\n2. \"Software deteriorates due to?\" → changes (maintenance), wear nahi.\n3. \"Software crisis\" → late, over budget, low quality projects (1960s).","tips":"- \"Always / never\" jaise absolute words pe shak karo — testing isi liye hoti hai.","sol":"A, B, C sahi characteristics; D (\"always correct\") galat.\n\n**Answer: (D) Software is always correct**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 1.1 (Nature of software: failure curves for hardware and software)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-21":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Cohesion best → worst: **Functional > Sequential > Communicational > Procedural > Temporal > Logical > Coincidental**. Procedural se better = Functional, Sequential, Communicational → A, B, D.","concept":"### Cohesion = ek module ke andar ke elements kitne \"ek hi kaam\" ke liye jude hain (high = achha)\nBest se worst (yaad karne ke liye: **F**unny **S**tudents **C**an **P**lay **T**ennis **L**ike **C**hampions):\n1. **Functional** — module sirf ek well-defined kaam karta hai (e.g. `computeSquareRoot`).\n2. **Sequential** — ek part ka output agle part ka input (pipeline).\n3. **Communicational** — saare parts **same input/output data** pe kaam karte hain.\n4. **Procedural** — parts ek fixed **order of execution** follow karte (control flow), data alag ho sakta.\n5. **Temporal** — parts **same time** pe chalte (e.g. initialization module).\n6. **Logical** — parts logically similar category ke (e.g. saare input functions ek module me, flag se choose).\n7. **Coincidental** — koi rishta nahi (random grouping) — sabse kharab.\n\n### Coupling = modules ke beech dependency (low = achha)\nBest (lowest) se worst (highest):\n1. **Data coupling** — sirf zaruri simple data parameters pass.\n2. **Stamp coupling** — poora data structure (record) pass, jabki thoda hi chahiye.\n3. **Control coupling** — control flag pass (dusre ka flow decide).\n4. **External coupling** — external format/device/protocol share.\n5. **Common coupling** — global data share.\n6. **Content coupling** — ek module doosre ke andar ka data/code seedha badle — sabse kharab.\n\n**Goal: High cohesion, low coupling.**\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Modules X, Y same input-output data pe operate\" → communicational cohesion.\n2. \"Increasing order of coupling\" → Data, Stamp, Control, External, Common, Content.\n3. \"Initialization module\" → temporal cohesion.\n4. \"Global variables se kaunsa coupling?\" → common.","tips":"- Cohesion me \"function, sequence, communication\" upar; \"time, logic, coincidence\" neeche.\n- Coupling me \"data → stamp → control → external → common → content\".","sol":"Procedural se better (upar wale): Functional (A), Sequential (B), Communicational (D). Temporal (C), Logical (E) usse kharab.\n\n**Answer: (B) A, B and D only**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 12.3 (Cohesion and coupling).\n- Rajib Mall — Fundamentals of Software Engineering, Sec 6.4 (Classification of cohesion and coupling)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-22":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Andar se bahar: (A+B) → **AB+**; ×D → **AB+D\\***; (E−F) → **EF−**; ↑ → **AB+D\\*EF−↑**.","concept":"### Infix, Prefix, Postfix\n- **Infix**: operator beech me (A + B). Parentheses/precedence chahiye.\n- **Prefix (Polish)**: operator pehle (+ A B).\n- **Postfix (Reverse Polish)**: operator baad me (A B +). Parentheses ki zarurat nahi; **stack** se evaluate.\n\n### Precedence aur associativity\n- ↑ / ^ (exponent): sabse high, **right associative** (A↑B↑C = A↑(B↑C)).\n- ×, /: next, left associative.\n- +, −: lowest, left associative.\n\n### Infix → postfix (stack algorithm)\n1. Operand → output me.\n2. '(' → stack pe push.\n3. ')' → '(' aane tak pop karke output.\n4. Operator → stack ke top pe jab tak higher/equal precedence (left-assoc) wala operator hai, pop karke output; phir push.\n5. End me stack khaali karo.\n\n### Postfix evaluation (stack)\nOperand push; operator aaye to top 2 pop (pehla pop = right operand), result push.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. A + B × C → A B C × +; prefix → + A × B C.\n2. Evaluate \"4 3 × 2 5 × + 8 −\" → 12 + 10 − 8 = 14.\n3. A ↑ B ↑ C → A B C ↑ ↑ (right associative).\n4. \"Postfix evaluate karne ke liye kaunsa data structure?\" → stack.","tips":"- Brackets andar se bahar solve karo — har solved part ek operand ban jaata hai.\n- Last operator (sabse bahar wala) postfix me **last** aata hai, prefix me **first**.","sol":"Q = ((A + B) × D) ↑ (E − F)\n1. A + B → AB+\n2. (AB+) × D → AB+D×\n3. E − F → EF−\n4. (AB+D×) ↑ (EF−) → **AB+D×EF−↑**\n\n**Answer: (D) AB+D*EF−↑**","refs":"- Seymour Lipschutz — Data Structures (Schaum's), Ch. 6 (Stacks: polish notation, evaluation, transformation).\n- Tanenbaum, Langsam, Augenstein — Data Structures Using C, Sec 3.3 (Infix, postfix, prefix)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-23":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Height-balanced** = height hamesha O(log n) guarantee: **AVL, Red-Black, B-tree** ✓. Normal BST skewed ho kar O(n) height ka ho sakta ✗.","concept":"### Binary Search Tree (BST)\nLeft < root < right. Search/insert/delete = O(h). Sorted order me insert karo to **skewed** (linked list jaisa) → h = n − 1 → O(n). Isliye BST khud balanced **nahi**.\n\n### Self-balancing trees\n- **AVL tree**: har node ka **balance factor** = height(left) − height(right) ∈ {−1, 0, 1}. Rotations: LL, RR, LR, RL. Height ≤ ~1.44 log₂ n. Strictly balanced → search fast, insert/delete pe zyada rotations.\n- **Red-Black tree**: har node red/black; root black; red ka child red nahi; har root-leaf path pe same number of black nodes. **Longest path ≤ 2 × shortest path**, height ≤ 2 log₂(n + 1). Kam rotations (insert ≤ 2, delete ≤ 3) — Java TreeMap, C++ map, Linux scheduler.\n- **B-tree** (order m): multiway, saare **leaves same level** pe; har node me ⌈m/2⌉−1 se m−1 keys. Disk-based indexing. B+ tree: data sirf leaves me, leaves linked.\n- **2-3 tree / 2-3-4 tree**: B-trees of order 3/4; 2-3-4 tree ≅ red-black tree.\n- Splay tree: amortized O(log n), strictly balanced nahi.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Path from root to furthest leaf ≤ 2 × nearest leaf\" → red-black tree.\n2. \"AVL vs RB\" → AVL zyada balanced (fast lookup), RB kam rotations (fast insert/delete).\n3. Minimum nodes in AVL of height h: N(h) = N(h−1) + N(h−2) + 1 → N(0)=1, N(1)=2, N(2)=4, N(3)=7.\n4. \"Insert 1,2,3 in AVL\" → RR rotation, root 2.","tips":"- BST ≠ balanced. \"Self-balancing\" naam wale (AVL, RB, B, 2-3, 2-3-4) balanced.","sol":"A (BST) ✗ — skewed ho sakta. B (AVL), C (Red-Black), D (B-tree) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (D) B, C and D only**","refs":"- Cormen (CLRS) — Ch. 12 (BST), Ch. 13 (Red-Black trees), Ch. 18 (B-trees).\n- Mark Allen Weiss — Data Structures and Algorithm Analysis, Ch. 4 (AVL trees), Ch. 12 (Red-black trees)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-24":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Bandwidth-delay product** = bandwidth × (round-trip) delay = 1 Mbps × 30 ms = 10⁶ × 0.03 = **30,000 bits**.","concept":"### Bandwidth-delay product (BDP)\nLink pe ek round trip ke dauraan kitne bits \"pipe\" me ho sakte = **bandwidth × delay**. Isse pata chalta hai sender ko ACK aane tak kitna data bhejte rehna chahiye (window size).\n\n### Stop-and-wait\n- Ek frame bhejo, ACK ka wait. Agar frame size < BDP → link khaali baitha rehta (waste).\n- **Efficiency/utilization** = frame size / BDP (jab round trip = 1 bit ka round trip):\n  = Tt / (Tt + 2Tp) = 1 / (1 + 2a), a = Tp / Tt.\n- Sliding window size W → efficiency = W / (1 + 2a) (max 1).\n\n### Forouzan ka example (yahi question)\n1 Mbps, 1 bit ka round trip 20 ms → BDP = 20,000 bits; frame 1000 bits → utilization = 1000/20000 = 5%. Yahan 30 ms → 30,000 bits.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Frame 1000 bits → utilization = 1000 / 30000 = 3.33%.\n2. Full utilization ke liye sliding window = 30,000 / 1000 = 30 frames (≈ 31 with 1 + 2a formula).\n3. Go-Back-N window ≤ 2ᵐ − 1, Selective Repeat ≤ 2ᵐ⁻¹ (m = sequence number bits).","tips":"- Question me \"round trip\" diya hai to direct multiply; agar one-way propagation diya ho to RTT = 2 × Tp.\n- Units: Mbps = 10⁶ bits/s, ms = 10⁻³ s.","sol":"BDP = 1 × 10⁶ bits/s × 30 × 10⁻³ s = **30,000 bits**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 30,000 bits**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch. 11 (Data link control: Stop-and-Wait, bandwidth-delay product example).\n- Kurose & Ross — Sec 3.4 (Pipelined reliable data transfer, utilization)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-25":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**McCall** ke 11 factors me **Maintainability, Usability, Integrity** hain; **Functionality** nahi (wo ISO 9126 / FURPS ka hai).","concept":"### McCall's quality factors (1977) — 3 perspectives, 11 factors\n- **Product operation**: Correctness, Reliability, Efficiency, **Integrity**, **Usability**.\n- **Product revision**: **Maintainability**, Flexibility, Testability.\n- **Product transition**: Portability, Reusability, Interoperability.\n\n### ISO 9126 (6): Functionality, Reliability, Usability, Efficiency, Maintainability, Portability.\n### FURPS (HP): Functionality, Usability, Reliability, Performance, Supportability.\n### Boehm's model: high-level → as-is utility, maintainability, portability.\n\n### Definitions (short)\n- **Integrity**: unauthorized access control.\n- **Usability**: seekhne/use karne ka effort.\n- **Testability**: test karne ka effort.\n- **Interoperability**: dusre system ke saath kaam karna.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Product transition factors\" → portability, reusability, interoperability.\n2. \"Which is in ISO 9126 but not McCall?\" → functionality.\n3. \"Correctness kis category me?\" → product operation.","tips":"- McCall yaad: **Operation (COREI-U)**, **Revision (MFT)**, **Transition (PRI)**.","sol":"A Maintainability ✓, B Usability ✓, C Integrity ✓, D Functionality ✗.\n\n**Answer: (D) A, B and C only**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 19.2.2 (McCall's quality factors)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-26":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pehle physical server pe **hypervisor install**, phir **VMs create**, phir unhe **resources allocate**, phir VMs **isolated workloads** chalate hain → A, B, C, D.","concept":"### Virtualization\nEk physical machine pe kai **virtual machines (VMs)** — har VM apna OS chalata hai, isolated.\n- **Hypervisor / VMM (Virtual Machine Monitor)**: VMs banata, manage karta, hardware share karwata.\n  - **Type 1 (bare-metal)**: seedha hardware pe (VMware ESXi, Xen, Hyper-V, KVM).\n  - **Type 2 (hosted)**: host OS ke upar (VirtualBox, VMware Workstation).\n- **Full virtualization** (guest OS unmodified), **para-virtualization** (guest OS modified, Xen), **hardware-assisted** (Intel VT-x, AMD-V).\n- **Containers** (Docker): OS-level virtualization, kernel share — VM se halke.\n\n### Cloud computing\n- Service models: **IaaS** (VMs, storage — AWS EC2), **PaaS** (platform to develop/deploy — Google App Engine), **SaaS** (ready software — Gmail).\n- Deployment: public, private, hybrid, community.\n- Characteristics (NIST): on-demand self-service, broad network access, **resource pooling**, rapid elasticity, measured service.\n- **SLA** (service level agreement): performance/availability ka contract.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Multiple OS on single physical server\" → virtualization.\n2. \"Development platform over Internet\" → PaaS.\n3. \"Hypervisor running on bare hardware\" → Type 1.\n4. \"Resource pooling/sharing kis concept se?\" → virtualization.","tips":"- Order hamesha: **foundation (hypervisor) → create → allocate → run**.","sol":"A (hypervisor install) → B (VMs create) → C (resources allocate) → D (isolated workloads run).\n\n**Answer: (B) A, B, C, D**","refs":"- Rajkumar Buyya, Vecchiola, Selvi — Mastering Cloud Computing, Ch. 3 (Virtualization).\n- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Ch. 18 (Virtual machines)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-27":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Inclusion–exclusion**: |div by 2 ∪ div by 5| = 100 + 40 − 20 = 120. Probability = 120/200 = **3/5**.","concept":"### Inclusion–exclusion principle\n- 2 sets: |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B|.\n- 3 sets: |A ∪ B ∪ C| = |A| + |B| + |C| − |A∩B| − |B∩C| − |A∩C| + |A∩B∩C|.\n\n### \"1..N me k se divisible\" = ⌊N / k⌋\nDivisible by both a and b = divisible by **lcm(a, b)**.\n\n### Probability\nP = favourable / total (equally likely).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Same for N = 100 → (50 + 20 − 10)/100 = 3/5.\n2. Divisible by 2 or 3 in 1..100 → 50 + 33 − 16 = 67.\n3. Not divisible by 2, 3 or 5 in 1..100 → 100 − (50 + 33 + 20 − 16 − 10 − 6 + 3) = 26.\n4. Odd or perfect square ≤ 100 → 50 + 10 − 5 = 55.","tips":"- \"Either A or B\" → union → inclusion–exclusion. \"Both\" → lcm.","sol":"- Div by 2: ⌊200/2⌋ = 100\n- Div by 5: ⌊200/5⌋ = 40\n- Div by 10: ⌊200/10⌋ = 20\n- Union = 100 + 40 − 20 = 120 → P = 120/200 = **3/5**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 3/5**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics, Sec 8.5 (Inclusion–exclusion), Sec 7.1 (probability; this exact example)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-28":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"p = team wins, q = raining. A: p → q = **conditional**. B: ¬p → ¬q = **inverse**. C: q → p = **converse**. D: ¬q → ¬p = **contrapositive**.","concept":"### Conditional statement p → q ke 4 roop\n- **Conditional**: p → q (\"If p then q\").\n- **Converse**: q → p (jagah badlo).\n- **Inverse**: ¬p → ¬q (dono negate).\n- **Contrapositive**: ¬q → ¬p (jagah badlo + negate).\n**p → q ≡ ¬q → ¬p** (contrapositive equivalent). **Converse ≡ inverse** (ek doosre ke contrapositive). Conditional aur converse equivalent **nahi**.\n\n### p → q ke English forms\n\"if p then q\", \"p implies q\", \"q if p\", \"**p only if q**\", \"q whenever p\", \"**p is sufficient for q**\", \"**q is necessary for p**\", \"q unless ¬p\".\np → q ≡ ¬p ∨ q; false sirf jab p true aur q false.\n\n### Biconditional p ↔ q: \"p if and only if q\" — dono same truth value.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"It is necessary to be not intelligent in order to be happy\" → happy → ¬intelligent.\n2. \"Contrapositive of 'If it snows, I stay home'\" → \"If I don't stay home, it doesn't snow\".\n3. Negation of p → q → p ∧ ¬q.","tips":"- Yaad: **Converse = swap**, **Inverse = negate**, **Contrapositive = swap + negate**.","sol":"A → IV (conditional), B → I (inverse), C → II (converse), D → III (contrapositive).\n\n**Answer: (C) A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics, Sec 1.1.5 (Converse, contrapositive, inverse)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-29":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Hex ka har digit = **4 bits**: A = 1010, 0 = 0000, F = 1111 → (A0F)₁₆ = **1010 0000 1111**.","concept":"### Number systems\n- Binary (2), Octal (8), Decimal (10), Hexadecimal (16: 0–9, A=10 … F=15).\n- **Hex ↔ binary**: har hex digit = 4 bits (nibble). **Octal ↔ binary**: har octal digit = 3 bits.\n- Hex → octal: hex → binary → 3-bit groups (right se).\n- Decimal → base b: baar-baar b se divide, remainders ulte. Fraction: baar-baar b se multiply, integer parts seedhe.\n\n### Table (hex → 4 bits)\n0 0000, 1 0001, 2 0010, 3 0011, 4 0100, 5 0101, 6 0110, 7 0111, 8 1000, 9 1001, A 1010, B 1011, C 1100, D 1101, E 1110, F 1111.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. (A0F)₁₆ decimal → 10·256 + 0 + 15 = 2575.\n2. (A0F)₁₆ octal → 101 000 001 111 → (5017)₈.\n3. (D.C)₁₆ → 1101.1100 → octal 015.6 → (15.6)₈.\n4. 2's complement of 28 in 16-bit → 1111 1111 1110 0100.","tips":"- Leading zeros mat hatao beech ke digits me (0 → 0000).","sol":"A → 1010, 0 → 0000, F → 1111 → **101000001111**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 101000001111**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Digital Design, Ch. 1 (Number systems, octal and hexadecimal)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-30":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Linux interrupt protection levels (increasing priority): **User-mode programs → Kernel system service routines → Bottom-half handlers → Top-half handlers** = A → D → B → C.","concept":"### Interrupt handling in Linux (Galvin)\nInterrupt handler do hisson me:\n- **Top half** — interrupt aate hi chalta, **interrupts disabled** (ya same-type masked), sirf urgent kaam (acknowledge, data copy). Sabse high priority.\n- **Bottom half** — baaki kaam baad me (softirqs, tasklets, work queues), interrupts enabled. Top half se kam priority.\n\n### Protection levels (Galvin Fig: increasing priority)\n1. **User-mode programs** (preemptible) — sabse kam.\n2. **Kernel-system service routines** (preemptible).\n3. **Bottom-half interrupt handlers**.\n4. **Top-half interrupt handlers** — sabse zyada.\nKam-priority code ko higher-priority code interrupt kar sakta hai, ulta nahi.\n\n### Linux ke aur facts\n- Linux kernel **preemptive** (2.6 se), **monolithic** with **loadable modules**.\n- Process scheduler: **CFS** (Completely Fair Scheduler).\n- `clone()` — flags se decide kitna share (threads banane ke liye).\n- Passwords: `/etc/shadow` (hashes), user info `/etc/passwd`.\n- Journaling file systems (ext3/ext4): changes pehle journal (transaction log) me.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Linux me thread banane ka system call (sharing flags ke saath)\" → clone().\n2. \"User account info kahan?\" → /etc/passwd.\n3. \"Journaling kya?\" → file system updates ko transaction ki tarah log karna.","tips":"- Order logic: hardware ke paas wala (top half) sabse urgent, user program sabse kam.","sol":"A (user) → D (kernel services) → B (bottom half) → C (top half).\n\n**Answer: (D) A → D → B → C**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Ch. 20 (The Linux system: kernel synchronization, interrupt protection levels figure).\n- Robert Love — Linux Kernel Development, Ch. 7–8 (Interrupts, bottom halves)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-31":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Cohen–Sutherland** (Sutherland–Cohen) = **line clipping** algorithm (region codes). Seed fill / scan-line seed = area filling.","concept":"### Clipping = window ke bahar ka hissa kaat dena\n**Line clipping**\n- **Cohen–Sutherland**: har endpoint ko 4-bit **region code (outcode)** — TBRL (top, bottom, right, left).\n  - Dono codes 0000 → poori line andar (accept).\n  - Codes ka AND ≠ 0000 → poori bahar (reject).\n  - Warna intersection nikaal ke repeat.\n- **Liang–Barsky**: parametric, Cohen–Sutherland se fast.\n- **Mid-point subdivision**: line ko baar-baar aadha.\n- Cyrus–Beck: convex windows ke liye parametric.\n\n**Polygon clipping**\n- **Sutherland–Hodgman**: har window edge se polygon clip (convex polygons ke liye; concave me extra edges).\n- **Weiler–Atherton**: concave polygons ke liye.\n\n**Text clipping**, point clipping (x_min ≤ x ≤ x_max …).\n\n### Filling (clipping nahi)\nBoundary fill, flood fill (seed-based), scan-line polygon fill.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Region code of a point left-above window\" → 1001 (TBRL).\n2. \"Sutherland–Hodgman is used for\" → polygon clipping (convex).\n3. \"Concave polygon clipping\" → Weiler–Atherton.\n4. \"Cohen–Sutherland me trivially reject condition\" → code1 AND code2 ≠ 0.","tips":"- \"Cohen\" = line, \"Hodgman\" = polygon, \"Weiler\" = concave polygon.","sol":"Clipping technique = Sutherland–Cohen (line clipping). Baaki filling/transformations ke.\n\n**Answer: (C) Sutherland-Cohen**","refs":"- Hearn & Baker — Computer Graphics (C version), Sec 6.7 (Line clipping: Cohen-Sutherland, Liang-Barsky), 6.8 (Polygon clipping)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-32":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Basic COCOMO **organic**: **E = 2.4 (KLOC)^1.05** person-months; time D = 2.5 (E)^0.38.","concept":"### COCOMO (Constructive Cost Model, Boehm 1981)\nEffort E = a (KLOC)^b person-months, Development time D = c (E)^d months.\n\n- **Organic** (chhota team, familiar, flexible): a = **2.4**, b = **1.05**, c = 2.5, d = 0.38.\n- **Semi-detached**: a = 3.0, b = 1.12, c = 2.5, d = 0.35.\n- **Embedded** (tight hardware/real-time constraints): a = 3.6, b = 1.20, c = 2.5, d = 0.32.\n\n### Levels\n- **Basic** — sirf KLOC.\n- **Intermediate** — 15 **cost drivers** (EAF) se multiply: E = a(KLOC)^b × EAF (organic a = 3.2).\n- **Detailed** — phase-wise cost drivers.\n- **COCOMO II** — application composition, early design, post-architecture; object points / function points.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. 32 KLOC organic → E = 2.4 × 32^1.05 ≈ 91 PM; D = 2.5 × 91^0.38 ≈ 13.9 months; staff = E/D ≈ 6.5.\n2. \"Embedded mode exponent\" → 1.20.\n3. \"Size from effort & productivity\" → KLOC = E / (PM per KLOC).\n4. Productivity = KLOC / E.","tips":"- Exponents yaad: **1.05, 1.12, 1.20** (organic → embedded badhte).","sol":"Organic basic COCOMO: E = 2.4 (KLOC)^1.05 PM.\n\n**Answer: (A) E = 2.4 (KLOC)^1.05 PM**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Ch. 33 (Estimation: the COCOMO II model).\n- Rajib Mall — Fundamentals of Software Engineering, Sec 3.4 (COCOMO: basic, intermediate, complete)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-33":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Closure** nikaalo: EC⁺ = E, C → (E→A) A → (A→B) B → (EC→D) D → saare A, B, C, D, E. EC ke bina E aur C kahin RHS pe nahi aate → **EC key**.","concept":"### Keys\n- **Superkey**: attributes ka set jo har tuple uniquely identify kare (X⁺ = all attributes).\n- **Candidate key**: minimal superkey.\n- **Primary key**: chuna hua candidate key. **Prime attribute**: kisi candidate key ka hissa.\n\n### Attribute closure X⁺ (algorithm)\n1. X⁺ = X.\n2. Har FD Y → Z: agar Y ⊆ X⁺ to Z ko X⁺ me jodo.\n3. Jab tak change ho, repeat.\n\n### Candidate key dhundhne ki trick\n- Jo attribute **kisi FD ke RHS me kabhi nahi** aata, wo **har key me hona chahiye**.\n- Unse shuru karo, closure nikaalo; kam pade to baaki attributes jodo.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. R(A,B,C,D), A→B, B→C, C→D → key A.\n2. Number of superkeys: candidate keys AB, AC of 4 attrs → 4 + 4 − 2 = 6.\n3. \"Is AC a key?\" → AC⁺ = A, C, B (A→B) → D, E nahi → nahi.","tips":"- RHS me kabhi na aane wale attributes (yahan E, C) = key ka zaroori hissa.\n- (C→F me F relation me hi nahi hai — ignore.)","sol":"- E aur C kisi FD ke RHS me nahi → key me zaroori.\n- EC⁺: {E, C} → E→A: A → A→B: B → EC→D: D → {A, B, C, D, E} ✓.\n- CD⁺ = C, D (E nahi) ✗; AE⁺ = A, E, B (C nahi) ✗; AC⁺ = A, C, B ✗.\n\n**Answer: (B) EC**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 7.4.2 (Closure of attribute sets).\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Sec 15.1 (Inference rules, closure), 15.2 (Keys)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-34":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"(a) P → ¬Q. (b) neither…nor = ¬P ∧ ¬Q. (c) \"Happy hone ke liye not-intelligent **necessary**\" = Q → ¬P. (d) \"Not intelligent hona = unhappy hona\" = ¬P → ¬Q.","concept":"### English → propositional logic\n- \"not p\" → ¬p; \"p and q\", \"p but q\" → p ∧ q; \"p or q\" → p ∨ q.\n- \"**neither p nor q**\" → ¬p ∧ ¬q.\n- \"if p then q\", \"p only if q\", \"p is sufficient for q\", \"**q is necessary for p**\" → p → q.\n- \"**necessary to be X in order to be Y**\" → Y → X (Y ke liye X zaroori).\n- \"To be X is to be Y\" → X → Y.\n- \"p unless q\" → ¬q → p ≡ p ∨ q.\n- \"p if and only if q\" → p ↔ q.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"She is intelligent but not happy\" → P ∧ ¬Q.\n2. \"Being happy is sufficient for being intelligent\" → Q → P.\n3. \"Negation of 'some students like hockey'\" → \"every student dislikes hockey\" (∀x ¬L(x)).","tips":"- \"Necessary\" ke saath arrow **ulta** lagta hai (jo necessary hai wo RHS pe).","sol":"(a) P → ¬Q, (b) ¬P ∧ ¬Q, (c) Q → ¬P, (d) ¬P → ¬Q → option (C).\n\n**Answer: (C)**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics, Sec 1.1–1.2 (Propositional logic, translating English sentences)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-35":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"3 sets ka **inclusion–exclusion**: 65 + 45 + 42 − 20 − 25 − 15 + 8 = **100**.","concept":"### Inclusion–exclusion (3 sets)\n|A ∪ B ∪ C| = |A| + |B| + |C| − |A∩B| − |B∩C| − |A∩C| + |A∩B∩C|\n- Pehle jodne se do-do wale do baar gine → minus karo.\n- Teeno wala +3 −3 = 0 ho gaya → wapas +1.\n\n### Venn regions (only-one, only-two)\n- Only Rice = 65 − 20 − 25 + 8 = 28.\n- Only Rice & Bread = 20 − 8 = 12, etc.\n- None = Total − union = 120 − 100 = 20.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Kitne log kuch nahi khaate?\" → 120 − 100 = 20.\n2. \"Sirf ek item\" → only R + only B + only C = 28 + 18 + 10 = 56.\n3. \"Exactly two\" → 12 + 17 + 7 = 36.\n4. 40 professionals, 25 Python, 20 Java, 7 neither → both = 25 + 20 − 33 = 12.","tips":"- Formula ka pattern: **+ singles, − pairs, + triple**.","sol":"|R ∪ B ∪ C| = 65 + 45 + 42 − 20 − 25 − 15 + 8 = 152 − 60 + 8 = **100**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 100**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics, Sec 2.2 (Set operations), 8.5 (Inclusion–exclusion)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-36":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Perceptron rule: **wᵢ ← wᵢ + η (t − y) xᵢ**. Har sample pe y = 1 agar Σwx > 0, warna 0. S2 pe w₂ = 0.1, S3 pe w₁ = 0.1, S4 sahi → final **(0.1, 0.1)**.","concept":"### Perceptron (single neuron)\n- Net input: y_in = Σ wᵢxᵢ (+ bias b).\n- Output: step function — y = 1 if y_in > 0 (threshold), else 0.\n- **Learning rule** (Rosenblatt): wᵢ(new) = wᵢ(old) + η (t − y) xᵢ, b(new) = b + η (t − y).\n  - t = target, y = output, η = learning rate.\n  - Sahi output (t = y) → koi update nahi.\n- Perceptron sirf **linearly separable** functions seekh sakta (AND, OR, NAND, NOR) — **XOR nahi** (Minsky & Papert). XOR ke liye multi-layer network (hidden layer) + backpropagation.\n\n### Delta rule (Widrow-Hoff / LMS)\nΔw = η (t − y_in) x — continuous error, gradient descent. Perceptron rule step output pe kaam karta hai.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Which Boolean function cannot be learned by single-layer perceptron?\" → XOR (aur XNOR).\n2. Neuron weights 1,2,3,4, inputs 5,7,10,30, linear k = 3 → 3 × (5+14+30+120) = 507.\n3. \"Learning rule of perceptron is also called\" → perceptron learning rule (delta rule ADALINE ka).","tips":"- Table banao: sample, y_in, y, error, Δw — 4 rows me ho jaata hai.\n- y_in = 0 pe output 0 (kyunki \"> 0\" pe hi 1).","sol":"η = 0.1, w = (0, 0).\n- S1 (0,0), t = 0: y_in = 0 → y = 0 = t → no change.\n- S2 (0,1), t = 1: y_in = 0 → y = 0 → error 1 → w₂ = 0 + 0.1·1·1 = 0.1 → w = (0, 0.1).\n- S3 (1,0), t = 1: y_in = 0 → y = 0 → error 1 → w₁ = 0 + 0.1·1·1 = 0.1 → w = (0.1, 0.1).\n- S4 (1,1), t = 1: y_in = 0.2 > 0 → y = 1 = t → no change.\nFinal: w₁ = 0.1, w₂ = 0.1 (ye OR function seekh gaya).\n\n**Answer: (A) w1 = 0.1, w2 = 0.1**","refs":"- Laurene Fausett — Fundamentals of Neural Networks, Sec 2.3 (Perceptron learning rule).\n- S.N. Sivanandam & S.N. Deepa — Principles of Soft Computing, Ch. 3 (Perceptron networks)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-37":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Greedy = har step pe **local best choice**, kabhi wapas nahi. **Dijkstra, Kruskal, Huffman** greedy hain; **Bellman-Ford** dynamic programming (sab edges ko baar-baar relax).","concept":"### Algorithm design techniques\n- **Greedy**: har step pe abhi ka best choice; global optimum tab milta jab problem me **greedy-choice property** + **optimal substructure** ho.\n  - Examples: **Dijkstra** (shortest path, non-negative weights), **Prim**, **Kruskal** (MST), **Huffman coding**, fractional knapsack, activity selection, job sequencing with deadlines.\n- **Dynamic Programming (DP)**: overlapping subproblems + optimal substructure; table me store.\n  - Examples: **Bellman-Ford**, **Floyd-Warshall**, 0/1 knapsack, LCS, matrix chain multiplication, edit distance, TSP (Held-Karp).\n- **Divide & Conquer**: independent subproblems: merge sort, quick sort, binary search, Strassen, closest pair.\n- **Backtracking**: N-queens, sum of subsets, graph colouring, Hamiltonian cycle.\n- **Branch & Bound**: 0/1 knapsack, TSP, job assignment.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"0/1 knapsack greedy se solve?\" → nahi (DP/B&B); fractional → greedy.\n2. \"Floyd-Warshall kaunsi technique?\" → DP, O(V³).\n3. \"Which is not divide & conquer?\" → heap sort.\n4. \"8-queens\" → backtracking; \"closest pair\" → D&C; \"string edit\" → DP.","tips":"- Bellman-Ford ka naam \"Bellman\" (DP ka founder) — DP yaad rakhne ki trick.","sol":"A Dijkstra ✓ greedy, B Kruskal ✓, C Huffman ✓, D Bellman-Ford ✗ (DP).\n\n**Answer: (B) A, B and C only**","refs":"- Cormen (CLRS) — Ch. 15 (DP), Ch. 16 (Greedy: Huffman), Ch. 23 (Kruskal, Prim), Ch. 24 (Bellman-Ford, Dijkstra).\n- Horowitz, Sahni, Rajasekaran — Fundamentals of Computer Algorithms, Ch. 4 (Greedy), Ch. 5 (DP)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-38":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Congestion hone pe router/node **source ko seedha** ek special packet bhejta hai = **choke packet**.","concept":"### Congestion control techniques\n**Open-loop (prevention)**: retransmission policy, window policy, acknowledgment policy, discarding policy, admission policy.\n**Closed-loop (removal)**:\n- **Backpressure** — congested node upstream neighbour ko slow karne bolta; wo apne upstream ko… (hop-by-hop, source tak peeche phailta). Virtual-circuit networks me.\n- **Choke packet** — congested router **seedha source** ko packet bhejta \"slow down\". (ICMP source quench purana example.) Beech ke nodes ko warning nahi.\n- **Implicit signalling** — source khud delay/timeouts dekh ke anumaan lagaata (TCP).\n- **Explicit signalling** — data packets me hi bit set (ECN, Frame Relay FECN/BECN — forward/backward).\n\n### Traffic shaping\n- **Leaky bucket** — constant output rate (bursty traffic smooth).\n- **Token bucket** — tokens jama, burst allowed upto bucket size. Max burst time S = C / (M − ρ).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Backpressure vs choke\" → backpressure node-to-node upstream, choke router → source directly.\n2. Token bucket C = 16 Mb, ρ = 2 Mbps, M = 10 Mbps → S = 16 / (10 − 2) = 2 s.\n3. \"TCP congestion control me slow start, AIMD\" → implicit signalling.","tips":"- \"Sent **to the source**\" → choke. \"Neighbour to neighbour\" → backpressure.","sol":"Congestion batane ke liye node se source ko bheja gaya packet = choke packet.\n\n**Answer: (C) Choke**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch. 24 (Congestion control: backpressure, choke packet, implicit/explicit signalling).\n- Tanenbaum — Computer Networks, Sec 5.3 (Congestion control, choke packets)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-39":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Controlled access** = stations kis order me bhejein ye decide hota hai: **Reservation, Polling, Token passing**. TDMA = channelization, CSMA/CA = random access.","concept":"### Multiple access protocols (Forouzan classification)\n1. **Random access** (koi control nahi, collisions possible): **ALOHA** (pure, slotted), **CSMA**, **CSMA/CD** (Ethernet, wired), **CSMA/CA** (Wi-Fi, wireless).\n2. **Controlled access** (stations consult karte, collision nahi): **Reservation** (reservation frame me slot book), **Polling** (primary station secondaries se poochta — poll/select), **Token passing** (token ring/bus — jiske paas token wahi bheje).\n3. **Channelization** (bandwidth baanto): **FDMA** (frequency), **TDMA** (time slots), **CDMA** (orthogonal codes, Walsh tables).\n\n### Throughput (ALOHA)\n- Pure ALOHA: S = G e^(−2G), max 18.4% at G = 0.5.\n- Slotted ALOHA: S = G e^(−G), max 36.8% at G = 1.\n\n### CSMA persistence methods: 1-persistent, non-persistent, p-persistent.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"CDMA uses\" → orthogonal codes (Walsh table), TDMA nahi.\n2. \"CSMA/CD invented for\" → wired Ethernet (wireless me CSMA/CA).\n3. Minimum frame size for CSMA/CD: L = 2 × Tp × B (e.g. 20 Mbps, 25 μs → 1000 bits).","tips":"- Teen categories yaad: **Random (ALOHA/CSMA), Controlled (Reservation/Polling/Token), Channelization (FDMA/TDMA/CDMA)**.","sol":"A Reservation ✓, B Polling ✓, D Token passing ✓; C TDMA (channelization) ✗, E CSMA/CA (random) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (B) A, B and D only**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch. 12 (Multiple access: random, controlled, channelization)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-40":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"GA ka order: pehle **fitness calculate** (C), phir **parents select** (A), phir **crossover (p_c)** (D), phir **mutation (p_m)** (B).","concept":"### Simple Genetic Algorithm (Goldberg) — ek generation\n1. Population initialise (random chromosomes).\n2. **Fitness evaluate** har member ka.\n3. **Selection** — parents chuno (fitness ke hisaab se): roulette wheel, tournament, rank, Boltzmann.\n4. **Crossover** probability p_c (usually 0.6–0.9) se offspring.\n5. **Mutation** har locus (bit) pe probability p_m (chhoti, 0.001–0.01).\n6. New population se replace; termination check; repeat.\n\n### Exploration vs exploitation\n- Crossover → existing good solutions ko mix (exploitation).\n- Mutation → naye regions explore, diversity (premature convergence rokta).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Fitness f(x) = x² − 4x + 4 for {1.5, 2, 3, 4.5} → f = 0.25, 0, 1, 6.25 → maximization me 4.5 (6.25) select.\n2. \"Which is not a GA operator?\" → jaise \"deduction\".\n3. \"Encoding types\" → binary, real/value, permutation (TSP), tree (GP).","tips":"- Natural order: **Evaluate → Select → Crossover → Mutate**.","sol":"C (fitness) → A (select parents) → D (crossover) → B (mutation).\n\n**Answer: (B) C → A → D → B**","refs":"- David Goldberg — Genetic Algorithms in Search, Optimization and Machine Learning, Ch. 1 (Simple GA).\n- Sivanandam & Deepa — Introduction to Genetic Algorithms, Ch. 2–3."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-41":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Har option ko **dono** sequences me (order maintain karke) dhoondho. Sirf **2,3,2,1** dono me milta hai (length 4 = LCS length).","concept":"### Subsequence vs substring\n- **Subsequence**: elements same order me, beech me gap allowed (1,3,1 is subsequence of 1,2,3,2,4,1).\n- **Substring**: continuous.\n- **LCS** (Longest Common Subsequence): dono strings ka sabse lamba common subsequence.\n\n### DP solution (O(m·n))\nc[i][j] = LCS length of X[1..i], Y[1..j]:\n- X[i] = Y[j] → c[i][j] = c[i−1][j−1] + 1 (diagonal ↖)\n- warna → c[i][j] = max(c[i−1][j], c[i][j−1]) (upar ↑ ya left ←)\n- c[0][*] = c[*][0] = 0. Answer c[m][n]; string arrows follow karke peeche se banti.\n- Time Θ(mn), space Θ(mn) (sirf length chahiye to Θ(min(m,n))).\n- Brute force: 2ᵐ subsequences → exponential.\n\n### MCQ shortcut\nOption ko check: (1) X me subsequence? (2) Y me subsequence? (3) length sabse zyada?\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. LCS(\"ABCBDAB\", \"BDCABA\") = 4 (e.g. BCBA) — CLRS example.\n2. LCS(\"AXYT\", \"AYZX\") = 2 (AX ya AY).\n3. \"LCS kaunsi technique?\" → DP (optimal substructure + overlapping subproblems).\n4. Edit distance, longest palindromic subsequence (= LCS(S, reverse S)) — related DP.","tips":"- Sabse lamba option pehle check karo; agar wo fail ho to agla.\n- Ek LCS unique nahi hota (yahan 2,4,1,2 bhi length 4 hai) — option me jo valid hai wahi answer.","sol":"X = 1,2,3,2,4,1,2; Y = 2,4,3,1,2,1.\n- (D) 2,3,1,2,1 (length 5): X me 2,3,…,1,2 ke baad koi 1 nahi ✗.\n- (A) 2,1,2,3: X me 2,1,2 ke baad 3 nahi ✗.\n- (B) 1,3,2,1: Y me 1 (pos 4) ke baad 3 nahi ✗.\n- (C) 2,3,2,1: X → 2(2), 3(3), 2(4), 1(6) ✓; Y → 2(1), 3(3), 2(5), 1(6) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (C) 2,3,2,1**","refs":"- Cormen (CLRS) — Sec 15.4 (Longest common subsequence).\n- Horowitz, Sahni — Fundamentals of Computer Algorithms, Ch. 5 (Dynamic programming)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-42":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"KDD steps me **data transformation** hota hai (A ✓). Data warehouse = **historical, non-volatile** data, online (OLTP) DB nahi (B ✗). **K-means** within-cluster variance (SSE) minimize karta (D ✓). Pattern evaluation = interestingness measures (E ✓).","concept":"### KDD (Knowledge Discovery in Databases) process — Han & Kamber\n1. Data cleaning (noise, missing values hatao)\n2. Data integration (multiple sources jodo)\n3. Data selection\n4. **Data transformation** (normalization, aggregation)\n5. **Data mining** (patterns nikaalna)\n6. **Pattern evaluation** (interestingness measures se useful patterns chuno)\n7. Knowledge presentation (visualization)\n\n### Data warehouse (Inmon)\n\"Subject-oriented, integrated, **time-variant**, **non-volatile** collection of data for decision support\".\n- **OLTP** (online transaction processing): operational DB, current data, many short updates.\n- **OLAP** (online analytical processing): warehouse, historical data, complex queries. Operations: roll-up, drill-down, slice, dice, pivot.\n- Schemas: star, snowflake, fact constellation.\n\n### Mining tasks\n- **Classification** (supervised: decision tree, Naive Bayes, SVM) — label predict; accuracy ek evaluation **measure** hai, classification khud measure nahi.\n- **Clustering** (unsupervised): **K-means** — k centroids, har point nearest centroid ko, centroid = mean; objective = Σ‖x − μ‖² (within-cluster SSE) minimize. K-medoids (PAM) outliers ke liye robust. DBSCAN density-based.\n- **Association rules** (Apriori, FP-growth): support, confidence, lift.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Support(A→B) = P(A ∪ B transactions)/total; confidence = support(A∪B)/support(A).\n2. \"Drill-down\" = summary → detail; \"roll-up\" ulta.\n3. \"Data warehouse characteristics\" → subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant, non-volatile.\n4. \"Apriori property\" → frequent itemset ke saare subsets frequent.","tips":"- \"Online database\" sunte hi OLTP → warehouse nahi.","sol":"A ✓, B ✗, C ✗ (classification ek task hai, accuracy measure), D ✓, E ✓.\n\n**Answer: (B) A, D, E Only**","refs":"- Han, Kamber, Pei — Data Mining: Concepts and Techniques, Ch. 1 (KDD process), Ch. 4 (Data warehousing & OLAP), Ch. 10 (K-means)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-43":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"EP = **valid/invalid classes** (II), BVA = **limits/boundaries** (III), Cyclomatic = **independent paths** (I), Decision table = **conditions–actions combinations** (IV).","concept":"### Black-box testing (specification based)\n- **Equivalence partitioning**: input domain ko classes me baanto (valid, invalid); har class se ek test case. E.g. age 18–60 → classes <18, 18–60, >60.\n- **Boundary value analysis (BVA)**: errors boundaries pe zyada → min−1, min, min+1, max−1, max, max+1. n variables → 4n + 1 test cases (basic BVA); robust → 6n + 1.\n- **Decision table**: conditions × actions; har rule = conditions ka combination.\n- **Cause-effect graphing**, state transition testing.\n\n### White-box testing (structure based)\n- **Cyclomatic complexity** V(G) (McCabe) = E − N + 2P = (decision points) + 1 = number of regions (planar graph). = **independent paths** ki count = basis path test cases ki minimum number.\n- Statement / branch / path / condition coverage.\n- Data-flow testing, mutation testing.\n\n### Levels: unit → integration → system → acceptance (alpha, beta).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. CFG E = 9, N = 7 → V(G) = 9 − 7 + 2 = 4.\n2. Code with 3 `if` and 1 `while` → V(G) = 5.\n3. \"Testing without knowledge of code\" → black-box.\n4. BVA for 2 variables → 4·2 + 1 = 9 test cases.","tips":"- \"Paths\" = cyclomatic; \"limits/edges\" = BVA; \"classes\" = EP; \"combinations of conditions\" = decision table.","sol":"A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV.\n\n**Answer: (A)**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering, Ch. 23 (Basis path testing, cyclomatic complexity; equivalence partitioning, BVA).\n- Rajib Mall — Fundamentals of Software Engineering, Ch. 10 (Black-box and white-box testing)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-44":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Binary → Gray: **MSB same**, baaki har bit = (pichla binary bit) XOR (current binary bit). 8 = 1000 → **1100**.","concept":"### Gray code (reflected binary)\nLagatar do numbers me **sirf 1 bit** badalta (unit distance). Use: rotary encoders, K-map labels (00, 01, 11, 10), error kam.\n\n### Binary → Gray\ng₁ = b₁ (MSB), gᵢ = bᵢ₋₁ ⊕ bᵢ. Shortcut: **G = B ⊕ (B >> 1)**.\n### Gray → Binary\nb₁ = g₁, bᵢ = bᵢ₋₁ ⊕ gᵢ (pichla **binary** bit XOR current gray bit).\n\n### Table 0–15\n0 0000, 1 0001, 2 0011, 3 0010, 4 0110, 5 0111, 6 0101, 7 0100, 8 1100, 9 1101, 10 1111, 11 1110, 12 1010, 13 1011, 14 1001, 15 1000.\n\n### Other codes\n- **BCD (8421)**: har decimal digit 4 bits (10 valid, 1010–1111 invalid).\n- **Excess-3**: BCD + 0011 (self-complementing).\n- 2421 code self-complementing; Gray non-weighted.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Gray 1011 → binary: 1, 1⊕0=1, 1⊕1=0, 0⊕1=1 → 1101 = 13.\n2. Decimal 13 → Gray: 1101 ⊕ 0110 = 1011.\n3. Excess-3 of 5 → 1000.\n4. \"Unit distance code\" → Gray.","tips":"- Shortcut B ⊕ (B >> 1): 1000 ⊕ 0100 = 1100.","sol":"8 = 1000₂ → g = 1, 1⊕0 = 1, 0⊕0 = 0, 0⊕0 = 0 → **1100**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 1100**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Digital Design, Sec 1.7 (Binary codes: Gray code)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-45":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Chaining = **linked list at index** (III), Linear probing = **next slot** (I), Quadratic = **i² steps** (IV), Double hashing = **second hash function** (II).","concept":"### Hashing\nKey → h(k) → table index. Collision = do keys same index.\n\n### Collision resolution\n**1. Separate chaining** — har slot pe linked list. Load factor α = n/m (>1 ho sakta). Avg successful search = 1 + α/2, unsuccessful = 1 + α.\n**2. Open addressing** (sab table ke andar, α ≤ 1):\n- **Linear probing**: h(k, i) = (h(k) + i) mod m → **primary clustering**.\n- **Quadratic probing**: h(k, i) = (h(k) + c₁i + c₂i²) mod m → **secondary clustering**; poori table probe na ho sake.\n- **Double hashing**: h(k, i) = (h₁(k) + i·h₂(k)) mod m → clustering sabse kam; h₂(k) ≠ 0 aur m ke saath coprime.\n- Expected probes (uniform hashing): unsuccessful ≤ 1/(1 − α), successful ≤ (1/α) ln(1/(1 − α)).\n\n### Hash functions: division h(k) = k mod m (m prime, 2 ki power se door); multiplication h(k) = ⌊m (kA mod 1)⌋; mid-square; folding; universal hashing.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Keys 12, 18, 13, 2, 3, 23, 5, 15 in m = 10 linear probing h = k mod 10 → 12→2, 18→8, 13→3, 2→4 (2,3 full), 3→5, 23→6, 5→7, 15→9.\n2. \"Primary clustering kis me?\" → linear probing.\n3. α = 0.5 → unsuccessful probes ≤ 2.","tips":"- Naam me hi strategy: linear = +1, quadratic = +i², double = 2nd hash.","sol":"A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.\n\n**Answer: (C)**","refs":"- Cormen (CLRS) — Sec 11.2 (Chaining), 11.4 (Open addressing: linear, quadratic, double hashing)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-46":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"CPU ke nazariye se: **Read (input)** = device → memory/CPU, **Write (output)** = memory → device.","concept":"### I/O transfer modes (Mano)\n- **Programmed I/O**: CPU status flag baar-baar check karta (busy-wait) — CPU time waste.\n- **Interrupt-driven I/O**: device ready hone pe interrupt; CPU beech me doosra kaam.\n- **DMA (Direct Memory Access)**: DMA controller memory ↔ device seedha transfer, CPU sirf start/end pe. Modes: **burst**, **cycle stealing**, transparent. CPU bus grant karta (BR/BG signals).\n- **I/O processor (channel)**: alag processor I/O handle (selector, multiplexer channels).\n\n### Read / write naming\n- I/O **read / input**: device → CPU/memory.\n- I/O **write / output**: memory/CPU → device.\n(Memory read/write: memory ke perspective se.)\n\n### I/O mapping\n- **Isolated I/O**: alag I/O address space, IN/OUT instructions.\n- **Memory-mapped I/O**: device registers memory addresses pe; normal load/store.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"DMA me CPU kab involve?\" → transfer shuru aur khatam (interrupt) pe.\n2. \"Cycle stealing\" → DMA ek-ek word ke liye bus leta.\n3. \"Programmed I/O drawback\" → CPU busy waiting.","tips":"- Write = \"likhna\" device pe = output.","sol":"I/O write = data memory se I/O device ko transfer.\n\n**Answer: (B) Transfer data from memory to I/O device**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Ch. 11 (Input-output organization: modes of transfer, DMA)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-47":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"GA parent selection: **Roulette wheel, Tournament, Rank, Boltzmann, Stochastic universal sampling, Random**. \"Radial\" koi selection technique nahi.","concept":"### Parent selection techniques\n- **Roulette wheel (fitness proportionate)**: P(i) = fᵢ / Σf. Problem: ek bahut fit individual dominate → premature convergence.\n- **Stochastic Universal Sampling (SUS)**: ek spin, multiple equally spaced pointers.\n- **Tournament**: k random individuals, unme best select (k = tournament size, selection pressure control).\n- **Rank selection**: fitness ke bajaye rank se probability (fitness values bahut close/door hon tab).\n- **Boltzmann selection**: temperature (simulated annealing jaisa) se selection pressure badhta.\n- **Random selection**, **elitism** (best ko seedha next gen me).\n\n### Crossover types: single-point, two-point, uniform, arithmetic, PMX/order (permutations).\n### Mutation: bit flip, swap, scramble, inversion, random resetting.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Fitness 10, 20, 30, 40 → roulette P = 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4.\n2. \"Elitism kya?\" → best chromosomes bina change agli generation me.\n3. \"Which is not a crossover?\" → e.g. \"Boltzmann\" (wo selection hai).","tips":"- Selection list yaad: **R-S-T-R-B** (Roulette, SUS, Tournament, Rank, Boltzmann).","sol":"Tournament, Boltzmann, Rank — valid selection techniques; Radial nahi.\n\n**Answer: (A) Radial**","refs":"- Sivanandam & Deepa — Introduction to Genetic Algorithms, Ch. 3 (Selection operators).\n- David Goldberg — Genetic Algorithms in Search, Optimization and Machine Learning, Ch. 1, 4."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-48":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Lipschutz: adjacency matrix = **sequential representation**; adjacency list = **linked representation**.","concept":"### Graph representations\n**1. Adjacency matrix (sequential representation)**\n- n × n matrix, A[i][j] = 1 (ya weight) agar edge i→j.\n- Space Θ(V²); edge check O(1); saare neighbours O(V).\n- Undirected → symmetric. Dense graphs ke liye achha.\n- A^k[i][j] = i se j tak length-k walks ki count (path matrix).\n\n**2. Adjacency list (linked representation)**\n- Har vertex ki list of neighbours. Space Θ(V + E). Sparse graphs ke liye achha.\n- Undirected me total list entries = 2E; directed me E.\n\n**3. Incidence matrix**: V × E. **Edge list**.\n\n### Traversal complexities\nBFS/DFS: adjacency list → O(V + E); matrix → O(V²).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Space for adjacency list of undirected graph\" → Θ(V + 2E).\n2. \"Warshall's algorithm\" → path (transitive closure) matrix.\n3. \"Sparse graph ke liye best representation\" → adjacency list.","tips":"- Array based = sequential, pointer based = linked (Lipschutz terminology).","sol":"Adjacency matrix se graph store karna = sequential representation.\n\n**Answer: (D) Sequential Representation**","refs":"- Seymour Lipschutz — Data Structures (Schaum's), Ch. 8 (Graphs: sequential representation, linked representation)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-49":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Circular queue → **CPU round-robin scheduling**; Priority queue → **Dijkstra**; Deque → **palindrome check** (dono ends se compare); Simple queue → **print queue (FIFO)**.","concept":"### Queue types\n- **Simple (linear) queue**: FIFO; rear pe insert, front se delete. Array me \"false overflow\" (front ke aage jagah khaali par rear end pe).\n- **Circular queue**: rear (n−1) ke baad 0 pe wrap → space reuse. Full: (rear + 1) mod n == front; empty: front == −1 (ya count 0). Use: round-robin scheduling, buffers.\n- **Priority queue**: highest priority pehle nikalta; binary **heap** se: insert/delete O(log n). Use: Dijkstra, Prim, Huffman, event simulation, OS priority scheduling.\n- **Deque (double-ended)**: dono ends pe insert/delete. Input-restricted / output-restricted variants. Use: palindrome check, sliding window max, undo-redo, work stealing.\n\n### Applications summary\n- Stack: recursion, expression evaluation, undo, DFS.\n- Queue: BFS, print spooling, buffering.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Circular queue size 5, front = 3, rear = 1 → elements = (1 − 3 + 5) mod 5 + 1 = 4.\n2. \"BFS uses\" → queue; \"DFS uses\" → stack.\n3. \"Priority queue ka best implementation\" → heap.","tips":"- Round-robin \"circle\" me ghoomta → circular queue.","sol":"A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I.\n\n**Answer: (B)**","refs":"- Tanenbaum, Langsam, Augenstein — Data Structures Using C, Ch. 4 (Queues, priority queues).\n- Cormen (CLRS) — Sec 6.5 (Priority queues), 24.3 (Dijkstra)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-50":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"DFA ko NFA ki tarah bhi dekh sakte (har DFA ek NFA hai). Sahi NFA wo hai jo **bilkul same language** accept kare — option ke NFA pe kuch short strings chalaake DFA se compare karo (accept/reject same aana chahiye).","concept":"### DFA vs NFA\n- **DFA**: har state se har symbol pe **exactly ek** transition; ε nahi.\n- **NFA**: ek symbol pe 0, 1 ya kai transitions; ε-moves allowed (ε-NFA). String accept agar **koi bhi** path final state pe khatam ho.\n- **Power same**: har NFA ka equivalent DFA (subset construction) — n-state NFA → max 2ⁿ states DFA. Har DFA already ek NFA.\n\n### Do automata equivalent kaise check karein (MCQ way)\n1. Accepting/final states compare karo (double circle).\n2. Chhoti strings (length 1, 2, 3) ko dono pe chalaao — DFA accept kare to NFA bhi kare, aur ulta.\n3. Unreachable / dead states ignore (language pe asar nahi).\n4. Formally: dono ka minimal DFA banao → isomorphic hon to equivalent.\n\n### Subset construction (NFA → DFA)\nStart = ε-closure(q₀). Har naye set S aur symbol a: move(S, a) ka ε-closure = naya state. Jis set me koi NFA final state ho wo DFA ka final.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. NFA with 3 states → DFA max 2³ = 8 states.\n2. \"NFA for strings ending in ab\" → 3 states; DFA bhi 3 states.\n3. \"Strings whose 3rd last symbol is a\" → NFA 4 states, min DFA 2³ = 8 states.","tips":"- Option me final states (double circles) ka mismatch sabse jaldi galat option hata deta hai.\n- Yahan figure low-resolution hai; official/PW key (D) ke hisaab se answer diya gaya hai — exam me upar wala string-test method lagao.","sol":"Diye gaye DFA ke transitions ko states merge karke (equivalent behaviour wale states) chhote NFA me likhne par option (D) wala automaton same strings accept karta hai (PW key ke anusaar). Method: har option pe short strings (a, aa, ab, ac, aab, …) chalao aur DFA ke result se milao — jo option kisi string pe alag result de wo reject.\n\n**Answer: (D)**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Sec 2.3 (NFA, subset construction), 4.4 (Equivalence & minimization of automata).\n- Peter Linz — An Introduction to Formal Languages and Automata, Ch. 2."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-51":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Inputs 00 pe output: NAND = 1, NOR = 1, **XOR = 0**, XNOR = 1 → sirf XOR 1 nahi deta.","concept":"### Basic gates truth table (inputs 00, 01, 10, 11)\n- AND: 0 0 0 1\n- OR: 0 1 1 1\n- NAND: 1 1 1 0\n- NOR: 1 0 0 0\n- XOR: 0 1 1 0 (inputs different → 1)\n- XNOR: 1 0 0 1 (inputs same → 1, equality detector)\n\n### Universal gates\nNAND aur NOR — inse koi bhi gate ban sakta.\n- NOT from NAND: dono inputs jod do (1 gate).\n- AND from NAND: 2 gates; OR from NAND: 3 gates; XOR from NAND: **4** gates; XOR from NOR: 5 gates.\n\n### Properties\n- XOR: A ⊕ 0 = A, A ⊕ 1 = A', A ⊕ A = 0; associative, commutative. Odd number of 1s → 1 (parity generator).\n- XNOR = (A ⊕ B)'.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Gate output 1 only when both inputs 0\" → NOR.\n2. \"Minimum NAND gates for XOR\" → 4.\n3. \"Which gate is used as parity checker?\" → XOR.","tips":"- 00 input pe sirf AND, OR, XOR = 0; NAND, NOR, XNOR = 1.","sol":"NAND(0,0) = 1, NOR(0,0) = 1, XOR(0,0) = 0, XNOR(0,0) = 1 → sirf C.\n\n**Answer: (D) C Only**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Digital Design, Sec 2.7–2.8 (Digital logic gates, NAND/NOR implementation)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-52":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"T1 ka key **AD** (A, D kisi RHS pe nahi). **D → E** = key ke hisse se non-prime → **partial dependency** → 2NF bhi nahi, 3NF bhi nahi. T2 ka key C; A → B transitive → 3NF nahi.","concept":"### Normal forms (quick rules)\n- **1NF**: atomic values.\n- **2NF**: 1NF + koi **partial dependency** nahi (candidate key ka proper subset → non-prime attribute).\n- **3NF**: har non-trivial X → A me **X superkey** ya **A prime**. (Transitive dependency nahi.)\n- **BCNF**: har non-trivial X → A me **X superkey**.\n- BCNF ⊂ 3NF ⊂ 2NF ⊂ 1NF.\n\n### Steps\n1. Candidate keys (closure se).\n2. Prime attributes mark.\n3. Har FD check: LHS superkey? nahi to RHS prime? nahi to violation; LHS key ka proper part → partial (2NF violation), warna transitive (3NF violation).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. R(A,B,C,D), AB → C, C → D, key AB → C → D transitive → 2NF hai, 3NF nahi.\n2. R(A,B,C), AB → C, C → B → keys AB, AC; sab prime → 3NF, par C not superkey → BCNF nahi.\n3. \"Highest normal form\" type questions — upar se BCNF check karte neeche aao.","tips":"- RHS me kabhi na aane wale attributes key me zaroor.","sol":"**T1(A,B,C,D,E)**: A, D RHS me nahi → AD⁺ = A, D, E (D→E), B (EA→B), C (EB→C) = all → key **AD** (only key). Prime: A, D.\nD → E: D key ka part, E non-prime → partial → T1 not 2NF → **not 3NF** ✓ (C true; A, D false).\n**T2(A,B,C,D)**: key C (C⁺ = C, A, B, D). A → B: A superkey nahi, B non-prime → not 3NF (B false).\n\n**Answer: (C) T1 is not in 3NF**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 7.3 (BCNF, 3NF).\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Sec 14.3–14.5 (2NF, 3NF, BCNF)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-53":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"(xy)² = x²y² → xyxy = xxyy → left me x, right me y cancel → **yx = xy** → abelian ✓. x² = e wala bhi abelian hota, par x³ = e / x⁵ = e se abelian guarantee nahi.","concept":"### Group (G, *)\nClosure, associativity, identity e, har element ka inverse. Commutative bhi ho to **abelian**.\n\n### Abelian hone ki standard conditions\n1. (ab)² = a²b² ∀a, b → abelian (proof: abab = aabb → cancel → ba = ab).\n2. (ab)⁻¹ = a⁻¹b⁻¹ ∀a, b → abelian.\n3. a² = e ∀a (har element apna inverse) → abelian.\n4. **Cyclic group** → abelian (converse false: Klein 4-group abelian, cyclic nahi).\n5. Order ≤ 5 ka har group abelian; order p² (p prime) bhi abelian. Smallest non-abelian = **S₃** (order 6).\n6. Abelian group ka **har subgroup abelian** (aur normal).\n\n### x³ = e kyun kaafi nahi\nHeisenberg group mod 3 (order 27) me har element ka order 3 hai par group non-abelian. Isi tarah exponent 5 ke non-abelian groups (order 125) exist karte.\n\n### Lagrange theorem\nFinite group me subgroup ka order |G| ko divide karta. Element ka order bhi |G| divide karta. Prime order group → cyclic.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"{1, −1, i, −i} under ×\" → cyclic (generator i), abelian.\n2. Group of order 6 ke possible subgroup orders → 1, 2, 3, 6.\n3. (Z₆, +₆) ke generators → 1, 5 (gcd(k, 6) = 1) → φ(6) = 2.\n4. \"Set of 2×2 invertible matrices under multiplication\" → non-abelian group.","tips":"- Cancellation law group me hamesha valid — (ab)² type questions me use karo.","sol":"(A) xyxy = xxyy → x⁻¹ left se, y⁻¹ right se → yx = xy → commutative ✓.\n(B), (C) — exponent 3 / 5 wale non-abelian groups exist → ✗.\n(D) commutative group ka subgroup bhi commutative hota → ✗.\n\n**Answer: (A)**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics (group theory appendix/exercises); I.N. Herstein — Topics in Algebra, Sec 2.3 (Abelian group lemmas).\n- J.P. Tremblay & R. Manohar — Discrete Mathematical Structures, Ch. 3 (Groups, subgroups, Lagrange)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-54":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Bada − chhota baar-baar = **Euclid ka subtraction GCD**. gcd(128, 110) = **2**.","concept":"### Euclid's algorithm\n- Subtraction version: jab tak x ≠ y: bade me se chhota ghatao → end me x = y = gcd.\n- Division version: gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b), gcd(a, 0) = a. Steps O(log min(a, b)).\n- lcm(a, b) = a·b / gcd(a, b).\n\n### do-while\nBody **kam se kam ek baar** chalti, condition baad me check.\n\n### Trace tip\nSubtraction GCD me x, y ka gcd kabhi nahi badalta (gcd(x, y) = gcd(x − y, y)) → seedha gcd nikaal lo.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Same code with x = 48, y = 18 → 6.\n2. Agar x == y shuru me (e.g. 5, 5) → do-while ek baar chalega: else branch y = 0 → while (5 != 0) → x = 5 − 0 … **infinite loop**. (Isliye do-while vs while ka farq poochha jaata.)\n3. gcd(1071, 462) = 21 (division method).","tips":"- Pehle confirm karo loop terminate hota hai (x ≠ y initially) — phir gcd.","sol":"128 − 110 = 18 → (18, 110) → y: 92, 74, 56, 38, 20, 2 → (18, 2) → x: 16, 14, …, 2 → x = y = 2. Output **2** (= gcd(128, 110)).\n\n**Answer: (B) 2**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 3.6 (do-while).\n- Cormen (CLRS) — Sec 31.2 (Greatest common divisor, Euclid's algorithm)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-55":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Real order: **URL type (C) → DNS resolution (A) → IP mila (D) → HTTP request (B) → page display (E)**.","concept":"### Browser me URL daalne ke baad kya hota (end-to-end)\n1. URL parse (scheme https, host, path).\n2. **DNS resolution**: browser cache → OS cache → local resolver (ISP) → root → TLD (.com) → authoritative server → **IP address**. (Recursive vs iterative queries; DNS mostly **UDP port 53**.)\n3. **TCP connection** (3-way handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) to port 80/443; HTTPS me **TLS handshake**.\n4. **HTTP request** (GET /path, headers, cookies).\n5. Server **HTTP response** (status 200/301/404…, HTML).\n6. Browser **render**: HTML parse → DOM, CSS → CSSOM, JS, extra resources → page display.\n\n### HTTP basics\n- Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, HEAD, OPTIONS, PATCH.\n- Status: 1xx info, 2xx success, 3xx redirect, 4xx client error (404), 5xx server error.\n- HTTP/1.0 non-persistent, 1.1 persistent + pipelining, HTTP/2 multiplexing, HTTP/3 over QUIC (UDP).\n- Stateless → cookies/sessions.\n\n### Ports: HTTP 80, HTTPS 443, FTP 20/21, SSH 22, Telnet 23, SMTP 25, DNS 53, POP3 110, IMAP 143, SNMP 161.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"DNS uses which transport?\" → UDP (zone transfer TCP).\n2. \"Non-persistent HTTP me 1 page + 10 objects → kitne TCP connections\" → 11.\n3. RTT calculation: non-persistent → 2 RTT per object + transmission.","tips":"- Logic: naam → address → connection → request → response.","sol":"C → A → D → B → E.\n\n**Answer: (D) C, A, D, B, E**","refs":"- Kurose & Ross — Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach, Sec 2.2 (HTTP), 2.4 (DNS).\n- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch. 26 (WWW and HTTP), Ch. 26/19 (DNS)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-56":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Clustered PT → **sparse address spaces** (III); Hierarchical PT → **64-bit ke liye inappropriate** (I, bahut levels); Segmentation → **user view** (IV); Inverted PT → **har physical frame ki ek entry** (II).","concept":"### Page table structures (Galvin)\n1. **Hierarchical (multilevel) paging**: page table ko bhi page karo (two-level, three-level). 32-bit ke liye theek, **64-bit me bahut levels** (7 tak) → \"generally considered inappropriate for 64-bit\".\n2. **Hashed page table**: virtual page number ka hash → chain of (VPN, frame) entries. 64-bit ke liye common. Variant: **clustered page table** — har entry kai pages (e.g. 16) ko refer kare → **sparse address spaces** ke liye useful.\n3. **Inverted page table**: system me **ek hi table**, **har physical frame ki ek entry** (pid, page). Memory bachti, par search slow (hash + TLB se fast); shared memory mushkil. (IBM PowerPC, UltraSPARC.)\n\n### Segmentation\nMemory ko **logical units** (main, stack, arrays, symbol table) me — **user's view of memory** support. Address = (segment no, offset); segment table: base, limit. External fragmentation hota. Paging = internal fragmentation.\n\n### Paging numericals\n- Logical address bits = page number bits + offset bits; offset = log₂(page size).\n- Page table size = (number of pages) × (entry size).\n- EAT with TLB = h(t + m) + (1 − h)(t + 2m).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. 32-bit VA, 4 KB page, 4 B PTE → 2²⁰ entries → 4 MB page table (isliye multilevel).\n2. \"Inverted page table ka size kis pe depend?\" → physical memory (frames) pe.\n3. TLB 20 ns, memory 100 ns, hit 80% → EAT = 0.8 × 120 + 0.2 × 220 = 140 ns.","tips":"- \"Each real page/frame\" = inverted. \"User view\" = segmentation. \"Sparse\" = clustered/hashed.","sol":"A → III, B → I, C → IV, D → II.\n\n**Answer: (B)**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 9.4 (Structure of the page table: hierarchical, hashed, inverted), Sec 9.2/8.4 (Segmentation)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-57":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Overloading = **compile-time polymorphism** (III); Early binding = **compile time resolve → fast** (II); Overriding = **run-time polymorphism** (IV); Late binding = **run time resolve → slow** (I).","concept":"### Polymorphism in C++\n**Compile-time (static, early binding)**\n- **Function overloading**: same naam, alag parameter list (number/type). Return type alone se overload nahi.\n- **Operator overloading**: `operator+` etc. (`::`, `.`, `.*`, `?:`, `sizeof` overload nahi ho sakte).\n- Templates.\n\n**Run-time (dynamic, late binding)**\n- **Function overriding**: derived class base ke **virtual** function ko same signature se redefine.\n- Base pointer/reference se call → actual object ka function (**vtable / vptr** mechanism).\n- **Pure virtual** (`virtual void f() = 0;`) → **abstract class** (object nahi bana sakte).\n- Virtual destructor — base pointer se delete karte waqt zaroori.\n\n### Binding\n- **Early (static)**: compiler call address fix → fast.\n- **Late (dynamic)**: runtime pe vtable lookup → thoda slow, flexible.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. `Base *p = new Derived; p->show();` — show virtual → Derived::show; non-virtual → Base::show.\n2. \"Abstract class\" → kam se kam ek pure virtual function.\n3. \"Which cannot be overloaded?\" → `::`, `.`, `?:`, `sizeof`.\n4. Java me saare non-static, non-final, non-private methods default virtual.","tips":"- \"Over**load**ing = **compile**\", \"Over**rid**ing = **run**time\" — overriding ke liye `virtual` chahiye.","sol":"A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I.\n\n**Answer: (C)**","refs":"- E. Balagurusamy — Object Oriented Programming with C++, Ch. 7 (Operator overloading), Ch. 9 (Pointers, virtual functions and polymorphism).\n- Bjarne Stroustrup — The C++ Programming Language, Ch. 20 (Derived classes, virtual functions)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-58":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Spinlock = **busy waiting** lock (A ✓). Ye **short** duration locks ke liye **useful** (B ✗), context switch **nahi** karta (C ✗), aur **multiprocessor** pe use hote (D ✗).","concept":"### Busy waiting vs blocking\n- **Spinlock**: lock na mile to process loop me ghoomta rehta (\"spins\") — CPU cycles waste.\n  - Fayda: **context switch nahi** → lock chhoti der ke liye ho to blocking se sasta.\n  - **Multiprocessor** pe useful: ek CPU spin karta, doosra CPU pe holder jaldi lock chhod deta.\n  - **Uniprocessor** pe bekaar: jab tak spinner chal raha, holder chal hi nahi sakta (preemption tak).\n- **Blocking semaphore**: wait() pe process **block** (waiting queue), signal() pe wakeup → context switches, par CPU waste nahi. Long waits ke liye.\n\n### Semaphore\n- wait(S) / P: S−−, agar S < 0 → block. signal(S) / V: S++, agar S ≤ 0 → ek process wakeup.\n- **Counting** semaphore (resources count), **binary** semaphore (mutex jaisa).\n- Negative value = waiting processes ki count.\n\n### Critical section solution requirements\nMutual exclusion, progress, bounded waiting. Peterson's solution (2 processes), hardware: test_and_set, compare_and_swap.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. S = 7, 20 P aur 15 V operations → 7 − 20 + 15 = 2.\n2. \"Mutex with busy waiting is called\" → spinlock.\n3. Producer–consumer: semaphores mutex = 1, empty = n, full = 0.\n4. \"Test-and-set lock bounded waiting satisfy karta?\" → nahi (simple version).","tips":"- \"Spin = ghoomte raho (busy wait), short lock, multi-CPU\".","sol":"Sirf A sahi.\n\n**Answer: (A) A Only**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 6.5 (Mutex locks, spinlocks), 6.6 (Semaphores)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-59":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"XML: users **apne tags** define karte (A ✓), **case sensitive** (C ✓), data ke **transport/storage** ke liye, presentation ke liye nahi (B ✗); key ke anusaar D (dynamic) bhi true.","concept":"### XML (eXtensible Markup Language) — W3C\n- **Data describe/store/transport** karta (HTML **display** karta).\n- **User-defined tags** (predefined nahi).\n- **Case sensitive** (`<Note>` ≠ `<note>`).\n- Har opening tag ka closing tag; proper nesting; **ek root element**; attribute values quotes me.\n- **Well-formed**: syntax rules follow. **Valid**: well-formed + DTD/XML Schema (XSD) ke against.\n- Related: **XSLT** (transform/presentation), **XPath** (navigate), XQuery, DOM/SAX parsers. CSS/XSL se display.\n\n### HTML vs XML\n- HTML: presentation, predefined tags, case-insensitive, kuch closing tags optional.\n- XML: data, custom tags, case-sensitive, strict syntax.\n\n### Web tech short\n- **DTD**: `<!ELEMENT note (to, from, body)>`.\n- JSON vs XML: JSON lighter.\n- Static (HTML) vs dynamic (server-side PHP/JSP/ASP, client-side JS) pages.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"XML document valid kab?\" → DTD/XSD ke according.\n2. \"XML namespace kyun?\" → naming conflicts avoid.\n3. \"SAX vs DOM\" → SAX event-based streaming (kam memory), DOM poora tree memory me.","tips":"- XML ke questions me \"presentation\" wala statement hamesha false (presentation XSL/CSS ka kaam).","sol":"A ✓, B ✗, C ✓, D ✓ (key) → A, C, D.\n\n**Answer: (C) A, C, D Only**","refs":"- W3C XML 1.0 Specification (well-formedness, case sensitivity).\n- Ivan Bayross — Web Enabled Commercial Application Development Using HTML, JavaScript, DHTML and PHP / XML chapters."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-60":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Intersection = **min(μA, μB)**; Bounded sum = **min(1, μA + μB)**; Bounded difference = **max(0, μA − μB)**; Algebraic sum = **μA + μB − μA·μB**.","concept":"### Fuzzy set\nHar element ki **membership** μ ∈ [0, 1] (crisp me sirf 0/1).\n\n### Standard operations (Zadeh)\n- Union: μ = **max**(μA, μB)\n- Intersection: μ = **min**(μA, μB)\n- Complement: μ = 1 − μA\n- Fuzzy sets **excluded middle / contradiction** follow nahi karte (A ∪ A' ≠ X, A ∩ A' ≠ ∅); De Morgan follow karte.\n\n### Algebraic / bounded operations\n- Algebraic sum (probabilistic OR): μA + μB − μA·μB\n- Algebraic product: μA · μB\n- Bounded sum: min(1, μA + μB)\n- Bounded difference: max(0, μA − μB)\n- Difference A − B = A ∩ B' = min(μA, 1 − μB).\n\n### Other concepts\n- **α-cut**: A_α = {x | μ(x) ≥ α} (crisp set). Strong α-cut: > α.\n- Support (μ > 0), core (μ = 1), height (max μ), normal set (height 1).\n- **Defuzzification**: centroid (centre of gravity), max-membership, mean of max, weighted average.\n- Fuzzy relation composition: **max-min**, max-product.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. A = {0.2/x1, 0.7/x2}, B = {0.5/x1, 0.4/x2} → A ∪ B = {0.5, 0.7}, A ∩ B = {0.2, 0.4}, algebraic sum x1 = 0.2 + 0.5 − 0.1 = 0.6.\n2. Bounded sum x2 = min(1, 1.1) = 1; bounded difference x1 = max(0, −0.3) = 0.\n3. α-cut at 0.5 of {0.2/a, 0.6/b, 1/c} → {b, c}.","tips":"- \"Bounded\" = 0 aur 1 ke beech clip karna (min 1 / max 0).","sol":"List II: I = μA + μB − μAμB, II = max(0, μA − μB), III = min(1, μA + μB), IV = min(μA, μB).\nA (intersection) → IV, B (bounded sum) → III, C (bounded difference) → II, D (algebraic sum) → I.\n\n**Answer: (C) A → IV, B → III, C → II, D → I**","refs":"- Timothy J. Ross — Fuzzy Logic with Engineering Applications, Ch. 2 (Fuzzy set operations), Ch. 4 (λ-cuts, defuzzification).\n- Sivanandam & Deepa — Principles of Soft Computing, Ch. 7 (Fuzzy sets)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-61":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Dedicated path** = circuit switching ki property. Packet switching me packets alag-alag routes le sakte, path reserve nahi hota.","concept":"### Switching techniques\n**1. Circuit switching** (telephone network)\n- Phases: **connection setup → data transfer → teardown**.\n- **Dedicated path** poori call ke liye reserve; bandwidth waste jab silent.\n- Delay = setup + transmission + propagation + teardown; data in-order.\n\n**2. Packet switching**\n- Data → **packets**, store-and-forward, path reserve nahi, **dynamic routing**, statistical multiplexing.\n- **Datagram** approach: har packet independent route (IP, connectionless) → out-of-order possible.\n- **Virtual circuit** approach: setup phase, saare packets same path, VCI label (ATM, Frame Relay, X.25, MPLS).\n\n**3. Message switching**: poora message store-and-forward (purana telegraph).\n\n### Delays (packet switching)\nTotal = transmission (L/R per hop) + propagation + queuing + processing. N hops, P packets: (N + P − 1)·(L/R) (pipelining) + propagation.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Resources reserved in setup phase\" → circuit switching.\n2. \"Internet uses\" → datagram packet switching.\n3. 3 hops, 5 packets, each 1 ms transmission → (3 + 5 − 1) × 1 = 7 ms.","tips":"- \"Dedicated / reserved\" = circuit. \"Different routes\" = datagram.","sol":"Dedicated path packet switching ki characteristic nahi.\n\n**Answer: (C) Requires a dedicated path**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch. 8 (Switching: circuit, packet, virtual-circuit)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-62":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Ek transfer = 15/30 = 0.5 s + 12 ms latency = **512 ms**. Swap out + swap in = 2 × 512 = **1024 ms = 1.024 s**.","concept":"### Swapping\nProcess ko main memory se **backing store** (disk) pe bhejna (swap out) aur wapas laana (swap in). Medium-term scheduler.\n- Swap time ka major part = **transfer time** ∝ process size.\n- Transfer time = size / transfer rate; + average latency (+ seek, agar ho).\n- Context switch time (swapping wala) = swap out + swap in = 2 × (transfer + latency).\n- Modern OS: poore process ki jagah **pages** swap (paging), standard swapping rare.\n\n### Disk access time (general)\nAccess time = seek time + rotational latency + transfer time (+ controller overhead).\n- Avg rotational latency = ½ × (60 / RPM) s.\n- Transfer time = data / transfer rate.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Galvin example: 100 MB, 50 MB/s, latency 0 → 2 s one way, 4 s total.\n2. 7200 RPM → rotation 8.33 ms → avg latency 4.17 ms.\n3. \"Why swap only idle processes?\" → pending I/O wale process swap karne se problem (I/O buffers).","tips":"- \"Total swap time (in + out)\" → **2 ×** zaroor.\n- MB aur MBps same units → seconds direct.","sol":"One way = 15 MB / 30 MBps + 12 ms = 0.5 s + 0.012 s = 0.512 s. Total = 2 × 0.512 = **1.024 s**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 1.024 sec**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 9.5 / 8.2 (Swapping: swap-time example)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-63":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Oblique = projectors plane se **90° nahi** (IV); Cavalier = perpendicular lines **foreshortening nahi** (II); Cabinet = **foreshortened** (aadha) (I); Orthographic = projection **perpendicular** (III).","concept":"### Projections\n**Parallel projection** (projectors parallel, centre of projection infinity pe)\n- **Orthographic**: projectors plane ke **perpendicular**. Types: multiview (top, front, side) aur **axonometric** (isometric — teeno axes equally foreshortened, dimetric, trimetric).\n- **Oblique**: projectors plane se **angle ≠ 90°**.\n  - **Cavalier**: angle 45° → perpendicular lines ki **length same** (foreshortening factor 1).\n  - **Cabinet**: angle ≈ 63.4° (tan α = 2) → perpendicular lines **aadhi** (factor ½) → zyada realistic.\n\n**Perspective projection** (projectors ek point — centre of projection — pe milte)\n- Dur ki cheezein chhoti (realistic). Vanishing points: one-point, two-point, three-point.\n- Parallel lines (plane ke parallel na hon) vanishing point pe milti.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Isometric projection\" → axonometric, teeno axes 120° pe, equal foreshortening.\n2. \"Number of principal vanishing points in 2-point perspective\" → 2.\n3. \"Parallel projection preserves\" → relative proportions/parallel lines; perspective nahi.","tips":"- Cavalier = full length, Cabinet = half length (cabinet chhoti almari).","sol":"A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III.\n\n**Answer: (B)**","refs":"- Hearn & Baker — Computer Graphics (C version), Sec 12.3 (Parallel projections: orthographic, oblique, cavalier, cabinet; perspective)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-64":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"RAID 2 = **memory-style ECC (Hamming)**; RAID 3 = **bit-interleaved parity**; RAID 5 = **block-interleaved distributed parity**; RAID 6 = **P + Q redundancy**.","concept":"### RAID levels (Galvin)\n- **RAID 0**: block striping, **no redundancy** (fast, risky).\n- **RAID 1**: **mirroring** (2× disks).\n- **RAID 2**: **memory-style error-correcting codes** (Hamming), bit-level.\n- **RAID 3**: **bit-interleaved parity** — ek parity disk; controller error detect kar leta.\n- **RAID 4**: **block-interleaved parity** — dedicated parity disk (bottleneck).\n- **RAID 5**: **block-interleaved distributed parity** — parity sab disks pe spread; 1 disk failure tolerate. Most common.\n- **RAID 6**: **P + Q redundancy** (Reed-Solomon) — **2 disk failures** tolerate.\n- **RAID 0+1 / 1+0 (10)**: striping + mirroring combos.\n\n### Capacity\nn disks of size D: RAID 0 = nD, RAID 1 = nD/2, RAID 5 = (n − 1)D, RAID 6 = (n − 2)D.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. 5 × 1 TB in RAID 5 → 4 TB usable; RAID 6 → 3 TB.\n2. \"Which RAID has no redundancy?\" → 0.\n3. \"Parity disk bottleneck\" → RAID 4 (RAID 5 me distributed hone se hata).","tips":"- 2 = Hamming, 3 = bit, 4 = block (dedicated), 5 = block distributed, 6 = P+Q (do failures).","sol":"A → IV, B → III, C → I, D → II.\n\n**Answer: (A)**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 11.8 / 10.7 (RAID structure, RAID levels)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-65":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"**Comma operator** left→right evaluate, value = **last** expression. a = 14, b = 7, a = 98, b = 91 → c = 91 → `--(*i)` = **90**.","concept":"### Comma operator\n- `(e1, e2, e3)` → e1, phir e2, phir e3 evaluate; poore expression ki value = **e3**.\n- **Lowest precedence** of all operators. Sequence point hai (har sub-expression ka side effect poora).\n- `x = (a = 2, a + 3);` → x = 5. Par `x = a = 2, a + 3;` → x = 2 (assignment comma se upar precedence).\n- Function arguments me comma **separator** hai, operator nahi.\n\n### Pointer + pre-decrement\n`--(*i)` → pehle *i (c) ko 1 kam karo, phir value use. `(*i)--` → purani value print, phir kam.\n\n### Note\nCode me `c=( … )` ke baad semicolon missing hai — asli compiler pe error aayega; exam intent ke hisaab se (semicolon maan ke) answer 90.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. `int x = (5, 10, 15);` → 15.\n2. `for (i = 0, j = 10; i < j; i++, j--)` — comma operator use.\n3. `int a = 1; a = a++ + ++a;` → undefined behaviour (sequence point ke bina do modifications).","tips":"- Comma expression me **har step trace** karo — beech ke assignments values badalte hain.","sol":"a = 12 + 2 = 14 → b = 14/2 = 7 → a = 14 × 7 = 98 → b = 98 − 7 = 91 → c = 91.\n`--(*i)` → c = 90 → print **90**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 90**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 3.5 (Comma operator), 5.1 (Pointers)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-66":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Elicitation = requirements **ikattha** karna (interview, use cases, FAST, brainstorming, QFD). **DFD** analysis/modeling tool hai, elicitation technique nahi.","concept":"### Requirements engineering tasks (Pressman)\nInception → **Elicitation** → Elaboration → Negotiation → Specification (**SRS**) → Validation → Management.\n\n### Elicitation techniques\n- **Interviews** (structured/unstructured).\n- **Questionnaires / surveys**.\n- **FAST** (Facilitated Application Specification Technique) — joint meeting customers + developers.\n- **Brainstorming**, **JAD** (Joint Application Development).\n- **Use case approach** (actors, scenarios).\n- **QFD** (Quality Function Deployment) — normal, expected, exciting requirements.\n- Prototyping, observation (ethnography), document analysis.\n\n### Analysis / modeling tools (elicitation nahi)\n**DFD** (data flow diagram, levels 0 = context diagram, 1, 2…), ER diagram, data dictionary, state diagrams, decision tables.\n\n### SRS characteristics (IEEE 830)\nCorrect, unambiguous, complete, consistent, ranked, verifiable, modifiable, traceable.\n\n### Requirement types\nFunctional (kya karna), non-functional (performance, security, usability), domain requirements.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Level-0 DFD is also called\" → context diagram.\n2. \"QFD ke requirement types\" → normal, expected, exciting.\n3. \"Which is non-functional?\" → response time < 2 s.","tips":"- \"Diagram\" (DFD/ERD) = modeling; \"baat-cheet/meeting\" = elicitation.","sol":"Interviews, use case, FAST — elicitation techniques. DFD — analysis modeling.\n\n**Answer: (D) Data Flow diagram**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering, Ch. 8 (Requirements engineering: eliciting requirements, FAST, QFD).\n- Rajib Mall — Fundamentals of Software Engineering, Ch. 4 (Requirements gathering & SRS)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-67":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Gantt chart banao. **SJF (non-preemptive)**: P3, P4, P1, **P5**, P2. **RR (q = 10)**: … **P5** (51–52), P2 (62). Dono me second last = **P5**.","concept":"### CPU scheduling formulas\n- **Turnaround time (TAT)** = completion − arrival.\n- **Waiting time (WT)** = TAT − burst.\n- **Response time** = first run − arrival.\n\n### Algorithms\n- **FCFS**: arrival order; convoy effect.\n- **SJF (non-preemptive)**: CPU free hone pe, **arrived** processes me sabse chhota burst. Optimal average WT (non-preemptive me).\n- **SRTF (preemptive SJF)**: naya chhota process aaye to preempt.\n- **Priority**: starvation → aging solution.\n- **Round Robin (q)**: ready queue FIFO; har process max q chalega, phir queue ke end me. **Convention**: same time pe naya arrival aur preempted process ho to naya arrival pehle queue me.\n  - q bahut bada → FCFS; bahut chhota → context switch overhead.\n- **Multilevel queue / feedback queue**.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Isi data pe SJF average WT: P3 0, P4 4 − 2 = 2, P1 12, P5 21 − 6 = 15, P2 32 → (0 + 2 + 12 + 15 + 32)/5 = 12.2.\n2. \"Starvation kis me?\" → SJF/SRTF, priority.\n3. \"RR me q → ∞\" → FCFS.","tips":"- RR me har step pe **queue ka state likho** — galti wahi hoti hai.","sol":"**SJF (non-preemptive)**:\n- t = 0: available P1 (9), P2 (30), P3 (4) → P3 0–4.\n- t = 4: P1 (9), P2 (30), P4 (8) → P4 4–12.\n- t = 12: P1 (9), P5 (11), P2 (30) → P1 12–21 → P5 21–32 → P2 32–62.\n- Finish order P3, P4, P1, **P5**, P2 → second last = **P5**.\n\n**RR (q = 10)**: queue P1, P2, P3 (P4 @2, P5 @6 baad me judte).\n- P1 0–9 (done). Queue: P2, P3, P4, P5.\n- P2 9–19 (rem 20) → queue: P3, P4, P5, P2.\n- P3 19–23 (done), P4 23–31 (done), P5 31–41 (rem 1), P2 41–51 (rem 10), P5 51–52 (done), P2 52–62 (done).\n- Finish order P1, P3, P4, **P5**, P2 → second last = **P5**.\n\n**Answer: (D) SJF: P5; RR: P5**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 5.3 (Scheduling algorithms: SJF, RR)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-68":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Legal: **ptr + int → ptr**, **ptr − int → ptr**, **ptr − ptr → integer** (same array). Illegal: ptr + ptr, ptr × / ÷.","concept":"### Pointer arithmetic in C\n- `p + n` → address p + n × sizeof(*p) (pointer).\n- `p − n` → pointer.\n- `p − q` (same array) → **integer** (elements ka difference, type ptrdiff_t), bytes nahi.\n- `p++`, `p--` valid.\n- Comparison (<, >, ==) same array ke pointers pe valid; NULL se == compare valid.\n- **Invalid**: p + q, p × n, p / n, p % n, bitwise ops on pointers.\n\n### Examples\n`int a[10]; int *p = &a[2], *q = &a[7];` → q − p = 5; `*(p + 3)` = a[5]; agar a = 1000 aur int 4 bytes → p + 3 = 1008 + 12 = 1020.\n\n### Arrays & pointers\n- `a[i]` ≡ `*(a + i)` ≡ `i[a]`.\n- Array naam ek constant pointer (a++ invalid).\n- `sizeof(a)` = poore array ka size, `sizeof(p)` = pointer ka size.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. `int *p = (int*)1000; p = p + 2;` (int 4 bytes) → 1008.\n2. `char *s = \"GATE\"; printf(\"%s\", s + 2);` → \"TE\".\n3. `int a[] = {10,20,30}; int *p = a; printf(\"%d\", *p++);` → 10 (post-increment pointer).","tips":"- Pointer ± number = pointer; pointer − pointer = number; pointer + pointer = illegal.","sol":"A (ptr + num → ptr) ✓, B (ptr − num → number) ✗ (pointer milta), C (ptr + ptr) ✗, D (ptr − ptr → ptr) ✗, E (ptr − ptr → number) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (D) A, E Only**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 5.4 (Address arithmetic)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-69":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Coupling low → high: **Data < Stamp < Control < External < Common < Content** → B, C, D, A, E.","concept":"### Coupling (recap + detail)\n1. **Data coupling** — sirf elementary data parameters.\n2. **Stamp coupling** — composite data structure (record/struct) pass, part hi use.\n3. **Control coupling** — flag pass jo doosre module ka logic control kare.\n4. **External coupling** — externally imposed format, communication protocol, device interface share.\n5. **Common coupling** — **global** data share (ek badla to sab affected).\n6. **Content coupling** — ek module doosre ke **internal data/code** ko seedha access/modify (ya beech me jump) — worst.\n\nCohesion ka order (best → worst): Functional, Sequential, Communicational, Procedural, Temporal, Logical, Coincidental.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Decreasing order of cohesion\" → functional … coincidental.\n2. \"Passing a whole employee record when only salary is needed\" → stamp coupling.\n3. \"Module A modifies a local variable of B\" → content coupling.","tips":"- Yaad: **D-S-C-E-C-C** (\"Data Stamp Control External Common Content\").","sol":"Stamp (B) → Control (C) → External (D) → Common (A) → Content (E).\n\n**Answer: (C) B, C, D, A, E**","refs":"- Rajib Mall — Fundamentals of Software Engineering, Sec 6.4 (Classification of coupling).\n- Pressman — Software Engineering, Sec 12.3."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-70":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"2 attributes {A, B}: possible FDs A → B / B → A — dono me LHS ek **key** ban jaata → **hamesha BCNF**.","concept":"### Facts about normal forms\n- **Binary relation (2 attributes) hamesha BCNF**: koi non-trivial FD ho to uska LHS key hai; koi FD nahi to key AB, sab trivial.\n- **Saare attributes prime** → **3NF** guaranteed (har FD ka RHS prime), lekin **BCNF zaroori nahi** (e.g. R(A,B,C), AB → C, C → B).\n- Relation me non-prime attribute ho, ye zaroori nahi (all-key relation).\n- **BCNF decomposition**: **lossless** hamesha possible, par **dependency preservation guarantee nahi**.\n- **3NF decomposition** (synthesis): lossless **aur** dependency preserving dono possible.\n\n### Lossless join test (binary)\nR → R1, R2 lossless iff (R1 ∩ R2) → R1 ya (R1 ∩ R2) → R2 (common attributes kisi ek ka superkey).\n\n### Dependency preservation\n(F1 ∪ F2)⁺ = F⁺.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. R(A,B,C), AB → C, C → B: keys AB, AC → 3NF yes, BCNF no; BCNF decomposition (C,B), (A,C) → AB → C lost.\n2. R(A,B,C,D) → R1(A,B), R2(C,D) → common nahi → lossy.\n3. \"Every relation in BCNF is in 3NF\" → true; converse false.","tips":"- Statement me \"always / every / preserves\" — BCNF ke liye dependency preservation wala hamesha **false**.","sol":"(A) ✓. (B) ✗ (sirf 3NF guarantee). (C) ✗ (all-key relations). (D) ✗ (BCNF dependency preserve na kare).\n\n**Answer: (A) A relation with only two attributes is always in BCNF**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 7.3 (BCNF and dependency preservation), 7.5.\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Sec 15.3 (Properties of decompositions)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-71":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pthreads = **POSIX standard IEEE 1003.1c** — thread creation **aur synchronization** ki API ka **specification**, implementation nahi.","concept":"### Thread libraries (Galvin)\n- **Pthreads**: POSIX (IEEE **1003.1c**) standard — **specification for thread behaviour, not an implementation**; OS designers jaise chahein implement karein. UNIX-type systems: Linux, macOS, Solaris, Tru64 UNIX. User-level ya kernel-level dono library ho sakti.\n  - `pthread_create()`, `pthread_join()`, `pthread_exit()`, `pthread_mutex_lock()`, `pthread_cond_wait()`.\n- **Windows threads**: kernel-level library (CreateThread).\n- **Java threads**: JVM ke upar, host OS ki library use (Thread class / Runnable).\n\n### Multithreading models\n- **Many-to-one**: kai user threads → 1 kernel thread (ek block → sab block; parallel nahi). Green threads.\n- **One-to-one**: har user thread ka kernel thread (Linux, Windows). Overhead zyada par concurrency.\n- **Many-to-many**: m user → n kernel (≤ m). **Two-level** model bhi.\n\n### User vs kernel threads\nUser threads: fast switching, kernel ko pata nahi. Kernel threads: OS schedule karta, blocking ek thread tak.\nThread share: code, data, files; apna: registers, stack, PC, thread ID.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Which is not shared among threads?\" → stack / registers.\n2. \"Many-to-one drawback\" → ek thread ke blocking system call se poora process block.\n3. `fork()` vs thread creation — thread sasta.","tips":"- Number yaad: **1003.1c** (1002 trap option hai).","sol":"(A) number galat (1003.1c). (B) sirf creation nahi, synchronization bhi. (C) ✓. (D) Tru64 UNIX bhi implement karta.\n\n**Answer: (C)**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 4.4.1 (Pthreads), 4.3 (Multithreading models)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-72":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"C precedence: **shift (>>)** > **relational (>=)** > **logical AND (&&)** > **conditional (?:)** > **assignment (+=)** → C, D, A, E, B.","concept":"### C operator precedence (high → low)\n1. `()` `[]` `->` `.` postfix `++ --`\n2. Unary: `! ~ ++ -- + - * & (type) sizeof` (right-to-left)\n3. `* / %`\n4. `+ -`\n5. `<< >>` (shift)\n6. `< <= > >=` (relational)\n7. `== !=`\n8. `&` (bitwise AND)\n9. `^`\n10. `|`\n11. `&&`\n12. `||`\n13. `?:` (right-to-left)\n14. `= += -= *= /=` … (right-to-left)\n15. `,` (comma)\n\nYaad karne ka mantra: **\"Unary, Arithmetic, Shift, Relational, Equality, Bitwise (& ^ |), Logical (&& ||), Conditional, Assignment, Comma\"**.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. `int x = 5 + 3 << 1;` → (5 + 3) << 1 = 16 (+ shift se upar).\n2. `a & b == c` → a & (b == c) (classic trap).\n3. `x = y = 5` → right associative.\n4. `!x > 0` → (!x) > 0.","tips":"- Shift arithmetic ke **neeche**, relational ke **upar**.","sol":"`>>` (5) > `>=` (6) > `&&` (11) > `?:` (13) > `+=` (14).\n\n**Answer: (C) C, D, A, E, B**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 2.12 (Precedence and order of evaluation, Table 2-1)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-73":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Instruction cycle: **Fetch → Decode → Effective address → Operand fetch → Execute** = B, C, A, D, E.","concept":"### Instruction cycle (Mano)\n1. **Fetch**: IR ← M[PC], PC ← PC + 1.\n2. **Decode**: opcode decode; addressing mode dekho.\n3. **Effective address** calculate (indirect ho to memory se address padho).\n4. **Operand fetch** memory se.\n5. **Execute**.\n(Phir interrupt check → agla cycle.)\n\n### Mano basic computer timing\n- T0: AR ← PC\n- T1: IR ← M[AR], PC ← PC + 1\n- T2: D0–D7 ← decode IR(12–14), AR ← IR(0–11), I ← IR(15)\n- T3: indirect ho to AR ← M[AR]\n- T4 onwards: execute.\n\n### Addressing modes (effective address)\nImmediate (operand instruction me), direct (EA = A), indirect (EA = M[A]), register, register indirect, **relative** (EA = PC + A), **indexed** (EA = XR + A), base register, auto-increment/decrement.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Memory references for indirect addressing operand\" → 2 (address + operand).\n2. \"Relative addressing used for\" → branch, position-independent code.\n3. \"Register used to hold address of next instruction\" → PC.","tips":"- Natural order: pehle instruction laao, samjho, address nikaalo, data laao, karo.","sol":"B → C → A → D → E.\n\n**Answer: (C) B, C, A, D, E**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 5.5 (Instruction cycle), Sec 8.5 (Addressing modes)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-74":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"λ-cut properties: (R ∪ S)λ = Rλ ∪ Sλ ✓, (R ∩ S)λ = Rλ ∩ Sλ ✓, λ ≤ β → Rβ ⊆ Rλ ✓. Complement: (R̄)λ ≠ (R̄λ) **except when λ = 0.5** — option me \"λ = 1\" likha → **invalid**.","concept":"### λ-cut (α-cut)\nFuzzy set/relation R ka λ-cut: R_λ = {x | μ_R(x) ≥ λ} — ek **crisp** set. (Strong cut: > λ.) Defuzzification ka simplest method — fuzzy ko crisp me badalna.\n\n### Properties (Ross)\n1. (A ∪ B)_λ = A_λ ∪ B_λ\n2. (A ∩ B)_λ = A_λ ∩ B_λ\n3. (Ā)_λ ≠ (A_λ)‾ **except for λ = 0.5** (complement λ-cut ke saath commute nahi karta)\n4. λ ≤ β (0 ≤ β ≤ 1) → A_β ⊆ A_λ (bada cut chhota set)\n5. A_0 = universe X (all elements).\nSame properties fuzzy relations ke liye.\n\n### Example\nA = {0.2/a, 0.5/b, 0.8/c, 1/d}: A_0.5 = {b, c, d}, A_0.8 = {c, d} ⊆ A_0.5 ✓.\n\n### Defuzzification methods\nMax membership, centroid (COG): z* = ∫μ(z)z dz / ∫μ(z)dz, weighted average, mean-max, centre of sums, centre of largest area, first/last of maxima.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. A_0.6 of {0.3/x1, 0.6/x2, 0.9/x3} → {x2, x3}.\n2. Weighted average defuzzification: Σ μᵢ zᵢ / Σ μᵢ.\n3. \"Fuzzy relation composition\" → max-min: μ(x,z) = max_y min(μR(x,y), μS(y,z)).","tips":"- Complement wali property me magic number **0.5** yaad rakho.","sol":"(A), (B), (D) valid properties. (C) \"except when λ = 1\" galat — sahi exception λ = 0.5.\n\n**Answer: (C)**","refs":"- Timothy J. Ross — Fuzzy Logic with Engineering Applications, Ch. 4 (Properties of λ-cuts for fuzzy sets and relations; defuzzification)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-75":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"DFA ke states **\"abc\" ke progress** ko track kar rahe: q0 → q1 (a) → q2 (b) → q3 (c, odd count). q3–q4–q5 phir agla \"abc\" dhundhte, aur c pe wapas q0 (even). Accepting q0 → **even number of \"abc\"**.","concept":"### DFA ka language kaise padhein\n1. **Start state** aur **final states** pehchano.\n2. States ka \"meaning\" socho — har state kya yaad rakh rahi hai (e.g. \"abhi tak 'ab' dekha\").\n3. Self-loops aur back-edges dekho: wo pattern toot-ne pe kahan wapas jaata.\n4. Options me chhoti strings test karo (ε, a, abc, abcabc, ab).\n\n### Substring count / parity DFAs\n- \"Contains abc\" → 4 states (progress 0, 1, 2, 3 + final trap).\n- \"Even number of abc occurrences\" → progress (0–2) × parity (2) = **6 states** — yahi q0–q5 hain.\n- \"Even number of a's\" → 2 states; \"a's even and b's even\" → 4 states.\n- \"Ends with ab\" → 3 states.\n\n### Regular expression ↔ DFA\n(a + b + c)* (abc)⁺ (a + b + c)* = \"contains abc at least once\" — ye parity nahi deta.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Min DFA states for strings over {a, b} with number of a's ≡ 0 mod 3 → 3.\n2. Strings containing \"aba\" → 4 states.\n3. Strings with |w| mod 3 = 0 and |w| mod 2 = 0 → 6 states.","tips":"- 6 states + \"abc\" ke 3 progress steps → turant \"parity × progress\" pattern socho.","sol":"q0 –a→ q1 –b→ q2 –c→ q3: pehla \"abc\" pura → odd region (q3, q4, q5). Waha phir a, b se progress aur c pe wapas q0 (even). Galat symbol pe progress reset hota hai (b, c loops). Start (and accepting) q0 = even count (0 included).\n- (A) (a* + b* + c*)⁺ = sab strings ✗. (B) at least one abc ✗ (abcabc reject nahi karna chahiye par zero wali bhi accept karni). (C) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (D) Even count (including 0) of substring 'abc'**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Ch. 2 (DFA design, product construction).\n- Peter Linz — An Introduction to Formal Languages and Automata, Ch. 2."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-76":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Type 3 (regular): **V → VΣ | Σ** (IV); Type 2 (CFG): **V → (V ∪ Σ)\\*** (I); Type 1 (CSG): **λVβ → λ(V ∪ Σ)⁺β** (II, context me); Type 0: **(V ∪ Σ)\\* → (V ∪ Σ)\\*** (III, unrestricted).","concept":"### Chomsky hierarchy\n- **Type 0**: unrestricted — α → β (α me kam se kam ek variable). **Recursively enumerable**. **Turing machine**.\n- **Type 1**: context-sensitive — αAβ → αγβ, γ ≠ ε (|LHS| ≤ |RHS|, non-contracting). **CSL**. **Linear bounded automaton (LBA)**.\n- **Type 2**: context-free — A → γ (LHS single variable). **CFL**. **PDA** (non-deterministic).\n- **Type 3**: regular — A → aB | a (right-linear) ya A → Ba | a (left-linear). **Regular**. **Finite automata**.\nType 3 ⊂ Type 2 ⊂ Type 1 ⊂ Type 0.\n\n### Closure (frequently asked)\n- Regular: union, concatenation, star, complement, intersection, reversal, homomorphism — sab closed.\n- CFL: union, concat, star closed; **intersection & complement nahi**. CFL ∩ regular = CFL.\n- DCFL: complement closed; union, intersection nahi.\n- CSL: complement closed (Immerman–Szelepcsényi), intersection closed.\n- Recursive: complement closed; RE: complement **nahi**.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. aⁿbⁿ → CFL (not regular); aⁿbⁿcⁿ → CSL (not CFL).\n2. \"Grammar S → aSb | ab type?\" → Type 2.\n3. \"Which machine for type 1?\" → LBA.\n4. \"Membership decidable for?\" → recursive (type 1, 2, 3) ✓, RE ✗.","tips":"- Number badhe → restriction badhe → language chhoti. Type 0 me koi restriction nahi.","sol":"A → IV, B → I, C → II, D → III.\n\n**Answer: (C)**","refs":"- Peter Linz — An Introduction to Formal Languages and Automata, Sec 11.4 (The Chomsky hierarchy).\n- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Ch. 9–10 (CSL, unrestricted grammars)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-77":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"A ✓ (conflict serializable definition), B ✓ (view serializable definition), C ✗ (serial me **interleaving nahi** hota), D ✓ (CS ⊂ VS).","concept":"### Schedules\n- **Serial**: ek transaction poora, phir agla (no interleaving). n transactions → n! serial schedules.\n- **Non-serial (concurrent)**: operations interleave.\n\n### Conflict serializability\n- **Conflicting operations**: different transactions, same data item, **kam se kam ek write** (R-W, W-R, W-W).\n- Swapping non-conflicting adjacent ops → conflict equivalent.\n- **Precedence graph**: Ti → Tj agar Ti ka op Tj ke conflicting op se pehle. **Cycle nahi → conflict serializable**; topological order = equivalent serial order.\n\n### View serializability\nView equivalent conditions: (1) same initial reads, (2) same reads-from (write-read), (3) same final writes.\n- Har conflict-serializable → view-serializable. Ulta nahi: **blind writes** (bina read ke write) wale schedules VS ho sakte par CS nahi.\n- VS testing NP-complete.\n\n### Recoverability\n- **Recoverable**: Tj ne Ti ka likha padha → Ti pehle commit.\n- **Cascadeless**: sirf committed data padho.\n- **Strict**: committed hone tak na padho na likho.\nStrict ⊂ cascadeless ⊂ recoverable.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. S: R1(A) W2(A) W1(A) W3(A) → CS nahi (cycle T1 ↔ T2) par VS (blind writes, final write T3) — classic.\n2. \"Precedence graph with cycle\" → not conflict serializable.\n3. \"2PL guarantees\" → conflict serializability (deadlock possible); strict 2PL → strict schedules.","tips":"- \"Chronologically interleaved\" = non-serial, serial nahi.","sol":"A ✓, B ✓, C ✗, D ✓.\n\n**Answer: (C) A, B, D Only**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 14.6–14.7 (Conflict & view serializability, testing).\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Sec 21.5 (Characterizing schedules based on serializability)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-78":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"DES: **64-bit block, 56-bit key** (B ✓). **RC4** = most common stream cipher (D ✓). Twofish key **upto 256 bits** (C ✗). Symmetric me algorithm public, **key secret** (A ✗ — Kerckhoffs).","concept":"### Symmetric (secret) key cryptography\nSame key se encrypt/decrypt. Algorithm public, **key secret** (Kerckhoffs's principle).\n- **DES**: block 64 bits, key 56 bits (64 with parity), **16 rounds** Feistel, round key 48 bits, S-boxes (6→4 bits). Weak (brute-force). **3DES**: 112/168-bit effective.\n- **AES (Rijndael)**: block 128, key 128/192/256, rounds 10/12/14. SubBytes, ShiftRows, MixColumns, AddRoundKey.\n- **Blowfish**: 64-bit block, key 32–448 bits. **Twofish**: 128-bit block, key **upto 256 bits** (AES finalist).\n- **IDEA**: 64-bit block, 128-bit key.\n- **RC4**: **stream cipher** (byte by byte, variable key) — WEP, old TLS. RC5/RC6 block ciphers.\n\n### Block cipher modes: ECB, CBC, CFB, OFB, CTR.\n\n### Asymmetric (public key)\n- **RSA**: n = p·q, φ = (p−1)(q−1), e·d ≡ 1 mod φ; C = Mᵉ mod n, M = Cᵈ mod n.\n- Diffie-Hellman key exchange, ElGamal, ECC.\n- Digital signature: sender ki **private** key se sign, receiver **public** key se verify.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. RSA p = 3, q = 11, e = 7 → φ = 20, d = 3 (7·3 = 21 ≡ 1); M = 2 → C = 2⁷ mod 33 = 29.\n2. \"Number of keys for n users: symmetric\" → n(n−1)/2; asymmetric → 2n.\n3. \"DES rounds\" → 16; \"AES-256 rounds\" → 14.","tips":"- Numbers yaad: DES 64/56/16, AES 128/(128,192,256)/(10,12,14).","sol":"A ✗, B ✓, C ✗ (256 bits), D ✓ → B, D.\n\n**Answer: (D) B, D Only**","refs":"- William Stallings — Cryptography and Network Security, Ch. 3 (DES), Ch. 5 (AES), Ch. 7 (Stream ciphers, RC4), Ch. 9 (RSA).\n- Forouzan — Cryptography and Network Security, Ch. 6–8."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-79":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Write-through = **cache + memory dono** update (A ✗). Write-back = sirf cache, memory baad me (B ✗). C ✓ (private caches + shared writable data). D ✓ (key ke anusaar — snoopy hardware solutions).","concept":"### Cache write policies\n- **Write-through**: har write cache **aur** main memory dono me. Simple, memory hamesha updated; traffic zyada (write buffer use).\n- **Write-back (copy-back)**: sirf cache update, **dirty bit** set; block replace hote waqt memory me likho. Traffic kam.\n- Write miss: **write-allocate** (block laao, mostly write-back ke saath) vs **no-write-allocate** (seedha memory, write-through ke saath).\n\n### Cache coherence (multiprocessor)\nProblem: har CPU ki **private cache** me same shared writable data ki copies → ek ne badla, doosre ke paas purana.\nSolutions (Mano):\n- **Software**: shared writable data ko non-cacheable mark; compiler/OS control.\n- **Hardware**: **snoopy cache controller** (bus pe writes watch, write-invalidate / write-update), **directory-based** protocols (scalable).\n- Protocols: **MSI, MESI** (Modified, Exclusive, Shared, Invalid), MOESI.\n\n### AMAT\nHierarchical: T = h·Tc + (1 − h)(Tc + Tm) ; simultaneous: h·Tc + (1 − h)Tm.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Tc = 10 ns, Tm = 100 ns, h = 0.9 (hierarchical) → 0.9·10 + 0.1·110 = 20 ns.\n2. \"MESI me E state\" → clean & sirf ek cache me.\n3. \"Dirty bit kis policy me?\" → write-back.","tips":"- A aur B ki definitions jaan-boojh kar ulti likhi hain — trap.","sol":"A ✗, B ✗, C ✓, D ✓ → C, D.\n\n**Answer: (C) C, D Only**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 12.6 (Cache memory: writing into cache), Sec 13.5 (Cache coherence).\n- Hennessy & Patterson — Computer Architecture: A Quantitative Approach, Ch. 5 (Snooping, directory protocols)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-80":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Kohonen SOM: winner (aur neighbours) ka weight input ki **taraf khiskta**: w(new) = w(old) + η (x − w(old)) = **(1 − η) w(old) + η x**.","concept":"### Kohonen Self-Organizing Map (SOM)\n- **Unsupervised**, **competitive learning** (\"winner takes all\").\n- Input layer fully connected to 2-D lattice (output layer).\n- Steps:\n  1. Weights random initialise.\n  2. Input x ke liye har neuron j ka distance D(j) = Σᵢ (wᵢⱼ − xᵢ)² (Euclidean).\n  3. **Winner (BMU)** = min distance wala neuron.\n  4. Winner + **neighbourhood** ke neurons update: **wᵢⱼ(new) = wᵢⱼ(old) + η [xᵢ − wᵢⱼ(old)]**.\n  5. η aur neighbourhood radius time ke saath ghatao. Repeat.\n- **Topology preserving** mapping (similar inputs paas-paas neurons pe). Clustering, visualization, dimensionality reduction.\n\n### Other learning rules (compare)\n- Hebb: Δw = η x y.\n- Perceptron: Δw = η (t − y) x.\n- Delta / Widrow-Hoff: Δw = η (t − y_in) x.\n- **LVQ** (supervised Kohonen): sahi class → w + η(x − w), galat → w − η(x − w).\n- ART (Adaptive Resonance Theory): stability-plasticity.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. w = (0.2, 0.6), x = (0, 1), η = 0.5 → w(new) = (0.1, 0.8).\n2. \"SOM learning type\" → unsupervised competitive.\n3. \"Hebbian rule formula\" → Δw = η x y.","tips":"- (1 − η)·w_old + η·x = weighted average of old weight and input — yaad rakhne ka asaan tareeka.","sol":"wᵢⱼ(new) = wᵢⱼ(old) + η (xᵢ − wᵢⱼ(old)) = (1 − η) wᵢⱼ(old) + η xᵢ → option (A).\n\n**Answer: (A) wij(new) = (1 − η) wij(old) + η·xi**","refs":"- Laurene Fausett — Fundamentals of Neural Networks, Sec 4.2 (Kohonen self-organizing maps).\n- Sivanandam & Deepa — Principles of Soft Computing, Ch. 5 (Unsupervised learning networks: Kohonen SOM)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-81":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"STRIPS = **action-centric** representation (A ✗ — feature-centric uska alternative hai). Features **primitive & derived** (B ✓). Action = **precondition + effect** (C ✓). **Conditional effects directly nahi** (D ✗).","concept":"### Planning in AI\nInitial state + goal + actions → action sequence (plan).\n\n### STRIPS (Stanford Research Institute Problem Solver, Fikes & Nilsson 1971)\n- **Action-centric** representation: har action ke liye batao uski precondition aur effect.\n- Features **primitive** (seedha actions se define) aur **derived** (primitive se rules ke through).\n- Action = **Precondition** (kab kar sakte) + **Effect** (add list + delete list).\n- **STRIPS assumption**: jo primitive feature action me mention nahi, wo **unchanged** (frame problem solution).\n- Conditional effects (\"agar X to Y\") seedha nahi — action ko split karna padta.\n- Poole & Mackworth: alternative = **feature-centric** representation (har feature ke liye rules: kaunse action use badalte).\n\n### Planning approaches\n- **Forward (progression) planning** — state space search from initial state.\n- **Regression (backward)** planning — goal se peeche.\n- **Partial-order planning** (least commitment).\n- **GraphPlan**, planning as **CSP/SAT**.\n- **Hierarchical Task Network (HTN)**.\n- PDDL — modern planning language.\n- Blocks world — standard example (pickup, putdown, stack, unstack).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"STRIPS action components\" → preconditions, add list, delete list.\n2. \"Sussman anomaly\" → blocks world me goal interaction (linear planner fail).\n3. \"Frame problem\" → kaun si cheezein action se nahi badalti — STRIPS assumption.","tips":"- STRIPS = \"Strips of actions\" → **action-centric**.","sol":"A ✗, B ✓, C ✓, D ✗ → B, C.\n\n**Answer: (B) B, C Only**","refs":"- Poole & Mackworth — Artificial Intelligence: Foundations of Computational Agents, Sec 6.1 (Representing states, actions and goals: STRIPS, feature-based).\n- Russell & Norvig — AI: A Modern Approach, Ch. 10/11 (Classical planning)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-82":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Master theorem: a = 2, b = 4 → n^(log₄2) = **n^½ = √n**, f(n) = √n → equal → **Case 2** → Θ(√n log n).","concept":"### Master theorem: T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n), a ≥ 1, b > 1\nCompare f(n) with **n^(log_b a)**:\n1. f(n) = O(n^(log_b a − ε)) → **T = Θ(n^(log_b a))**.\n2. f(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a) · logᵏ n) → **T = Θ(n^(log_b a) · logᵏ⁺¹ n)** (k = 0 → × log n).\n3. f(n) = Ω(n^(log_b a + ε)) + regularity a f(n/b) ≤ c f(n) → **T = Θ(f(n))**.\nMaster theorem lagu nahi: a < 1, non-polynomial gap (e.g. T = 2T(n/2) + n/log n), b ≤ 1.\n\n### Standard recurrences\n- T(n) = T(n/2) + 1 → Θ(log n) (binary search).\n- T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n → Θ(n log n) (merge sort).\n- T(n) = 2T(n/2) + 1 → Θ(n).\n- T(n) = T(n − 1) + n → Θ(n²); T(n) = 2T(n − 1) + 1 → Θ(2ⁿ) (Tower of Hanoi).\n- T(n) = 7T(n/2) + n² → Θ(n^2.81) (Strassen).\n- T(n) = T(√n) + 1 → Θ(log log n) (substitute n = 2ᵐ).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. T(n) = 4T(n/2) + n → Θ(n²).\n2. T(n) = 3T(n/4) + n log n → Case 3 → Θ(n log n).\n3. T(n) = 2T(n/4) + 1 → Θ(√n); 2T(n/4) + n → Θ(n).","tips":"- Pehle n^(log_b a) nikaalo, phir f(n) se compare — 30 second ka question.","sol":"n^(log₄2) = n^0.5 = √n = f(n) → Case 2 → T(n) = Θ(√n log n).\n\n**Answer: (C) Θ(√n log n)**","refs":"- Cormen (CLRS) — Sec 4.5 (The master method)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-83":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"CLRS ke 4 steps: **Characterize structure (C) → Recursively define value (B) → Compute value bottom-up (D) → Construct solution (A)**.","concept":"### DP ke 4 steps (CLRS)\n1. **Characterize** the structure of an optimal solution (optimal substructure).\n2. **Recursively define** the value of an optimal solution (recurrence).\n3. **Compute** the value, usually **bottom-up** (tabulation) ya top-down with **memoization**.\n4. **Construct** an optimal solution from computed information (traceback).\n\n### DP ke liye do properties\n- **Optimal substructure**: optimal solution me subproblems ke optimal solutions.\n- **Overlapping subproblems**: same subproblems baar-baar (D&C se farq — D&C me independent).\n\n### Classic DP problems & complexity\n- Fibonacci: O(n). LCS: O(mn). 0/1 knapsack: O(nW) (pseudo-polynomial).\n- Matrix chain multiplication: O(n³). Floyd–Warshall: O(V³). Bellman–Ford: O(VE).\n- Optimal BST: O(n³). TSP (Held–Karp): O(n² 2ⁿ). Rod cutting: O(n²). Edit distance: O(mn).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Matrix chain A(10×20), B(20×30), C(30×40): (AB)C = 6000 + 12000 = 18000; A(BC) = 24000 + 8000 = 32000 → 18000.\n2. Knapsack W = 5, items (w, v) = (2, 3), (3, 4), (4, 5) → max 7 (items 1, 2).\n3. \"Memoization = top-down / tabulation = bottom-up\".","tips":"- Logical order: samjho → equation → table → answer reconstruct.","sol":"C → B → D → A.\n\n**Answer: (D) C, B, D, A**","refs":"- Cormen (CLRS) — Ch. 15 introduction (Four steps of developing a DP algorithm)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-84":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Line coding (digital data → digital signal): **NRZ, Manchester, Bipolar AMI** ✓. AM = **modulation** (analog), Hamming = **error correction code**.","concept":"### Line coding schemes (Forouzan)\n**Unipolar**: NRZ (0 = 0 V, 1 = +V).\n**Polar**:\n- **NRZ-L** (level se bit), **NRZ-I** (1 pe transition/invert).\n- **RZ** (return to zero, beech me zero).\n- **Manchester**: har bit ke beech transition (IEEE: 0 = high→low, 1 = low→high; Thomas convention ulta) — self-clocking, bandwidth 2×. Ethernet 10 Mbps.\n- **Differential Manchester**: bit ke start pe transition = 0, no transition = 1; beech me hamesha transition. Token ring.\n**Bipolar**: **AMI** (0 = 0 V, 1 = alternate +/−), pseudoternary (ulta). DC component nahi.\n**Multilevel**: 2B1Q, 8B6T, 4D-PAM5. **Multitransition**: MLT-3.\n**Block coding**: 4B/5B, 8B/10B. **Scrambling**: B8ZS, HDB3 (long zeros ke liye).\n\n### Not line coding\n- **Modulation** (digital/analog → analog): ASK, FSK, PSK, QAM; AM, FM, PM.\n- **Error coding**: Hamming, CRC, parity.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Self-synchronizing scheme\" → Manchester / differential Manchester.\n2. \"Baud rate of Manchester for 10 Mbps\" → 20 Mbaud (signal rate 2 × bit rate).\n3. Hamming code bits for 7-bit data → 2^r ≥ m + r + 1 → r = 4.","tips":"- \"Encoding\" sun ke modulation/ECC mat chuno — sirf line coding.","sol":"A NRZ ✓, B Manchester ✓, C AM ✗, D Hamming ✗, E Bipolar AMI ✓.\n\n**Answer: (D) A, B and E Only**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 4.1 (Digital-to-digital conversion: line coding schemes)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-85":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Testing order: **Unit → Integration → System → Acceptance** = B, A, C, D.","concept":"### Levels of testing\n1. **Unit testing** — individual module (developer, white-box mostly). Drivers & stubs.\n2. **Integration testing** — modules jodkar interfaces test.\n   - **Top-down** (stubs chahiye), **bottom-up** (drivers chahiye), **big-bang**, **sandwich** (mixed).\n3. **System testing** — poora system vs SRS: functional + non-functional (performance, stress, volume, security, recovery, configuration, compatibility).\n4. **Acceptance testing** — customer: **alpha** (developer site pe, customer), **beta** (customer site pe, real users).\n\n### Other\n- **Regression testing**: change ke baad purane features check.\n- **Smoke testing**: build stable hai ya nahi (basic check).\n- **V-model**: har development phase ke saamne testing phase (requirements ↔ acceptance, design ↔ integration, coding ↔ unit).\n\n### Verification vs validation\n- Verification: \"Are we building the product **right**?\" (reviews, inspections).\n- Validation: \"Are we building the **right** product?\" (testing against user needs).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Stubs used in\" → top-down integration; \"drivers\" → bottom-up.\n2. \"Beta testing done by\" → end users at their site.\n3. \"Stress testing\" → system ko limits ke bahar load.","tips":"- Chhote se bade ki taraf: module → modules → system → customer.","sol":"B → A → C → D.\n\n**Answer: (B) B, A, C, D**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering, Ch. 22 (Software testing strategies: unit, integration, validation, system).\n- Rajib Mall — Fundamentals of Software Engineering, Ch. 10."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-86":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Huffman: **leaf nodes banao (B) → priority queue (min-heap) (C) → do sabse kam frequency nodes combine (D) → root banne tak repeat (A)**.","concept":"### Huffman coding (greedy, optimal prefix code)\n1. Har character ke liye leaf node (frequency ke saath).\n2. Sabko **min-priority queue** me daalo.\n3. Do minimum nikaalo, naya node = unka sum (left/right children), wapas queue me.\n4. n − 1 baar repeat → ek root bachta.\n5. Left edge = 0, right = 1 → root se leaf path = code.\nComplexity: **O(n log n)**.\n\n### Properties\n- **Prefix code** (koi code kisi doosre ka prefix nahi) → unambiguous decoding.\n- Zyada frequency → chhota code.\n- Average code length = Σ fᵢ·lᵢ / Σ fᵢ.\n- Total bits = Σ fᵢ·lᵢ = (sum of all internal node values).\n\n### Example\na:5, b:9, c:12, d:13, e:16, f:45 (CLRS) → codes f = 0, c = 100, d = 101, a = 1100, b = 1101, e = 111 → 224 bits (fixed 3-bit = 300).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Frequencies 1, 1, 2, 3, 5 → merges 2, 4, 7, 12 → total bits = 2 + 4 + 7 + 12 = 25.\n2. \"Huffman is which technique?\" → greedy.\n3. Max code length with n symbols → n − 1 (Fibonacci-like frequencies).","tips":"- \"Total bits\" shortcut: saare merged (internal) node values ka sum.","sol":"B → C → D → A.\n\n**Answer: (C) B, C, D, A**","refs":"- Cormen (CLRS) — Sec 16.3 (Huffman codes)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-87":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"(A) n ≥ 2 → mn > m ✓. (B) \"n > k\" galat ✗. (C) positive integers ka product 1 → dono 1 ✓. (D) composite k = mn with 1 < m, n < k ✓.","concept":"### Number theory basics\n- **Prime**: > 1, sirf 1 aur khud se divisible. **Composite**: > 1 aur prime nahi → k = m·n with **1 < m, n < k**.\n- 1 na prime na composite. 2 only even prime.\n- **Fundamental theorem of arithmetic**: har integer > 1 primes ka unique product.\n- Composite n ka ek prime factor ≤ √n (primality test: √n tak divide check).\n- Infinitely many primes (Euclid).\n\n### Divisibility\na | b → b = ak. a | b, a | c → a | (mb + nc). gcd, lcm; gcd(a, b)·lcm(a, b) = a·b.\nModular: a ≡ b (mod m) ↔ m | (a − b). Fermat: a^(p−1) ≡ 1 (mod p) (p ∤ a). Euler: a^φ(n) ≡ 1 (mod n).\n\n### Proof techniques (Rosen)\nDirect, contrapositive, contradiction, cases, induction. Ye statements aksar \"proof by ...\" questions me aate.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. φ(12) = 4 (1, 5, 7, 11).\n2. 3¹⁰⁰ mod 7: 3⁶ ≡ 1 → 3¹⁰⁰ = 3⁹⁶·3⁴ ≡ 81 mod 7 = 4.\n3. \"Is 97 prime?\" → √97 < 10, 2, 3, 5, 7 se divide nahi → prime.","tips":"- Statement me ek galat inequality (n > k) ho to pura statement false.","sol":"(A) n ≠ 1, n positive → n ≥ 2 → mn ≥ 2m > m ✓.\n(B) n > k impossible (n ≤ k) ✗.\n(C) m, n ≥ 1 aur mn = 1 → m = n = 1 ✓.\n(D) composite ki definition ✓.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (C), (D)**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics, Sec 4.3 (Primes), Ch. 1.7 (Proof methods)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-88":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Power: **LL(0)** sabse kam, phir **LR(0) < SLR(1) < LALR(1) < LR(1)** (canonical LR sabse powerful).","concept":"### Parsers\n**Top-down**: recursive descent (backtracking), **LL(1)** (predictive, no left recursion, left factored).\n**Bottom-up (shift-reduce)**: operator precedence, **LR(0), SLR(1), LALR(1), CLR(1) = LR(1)**.\n\n### Power order\nLL(0) < LR(0) < SLR(1) < LALR(1) < LR(1)\n- LL(1) ⊂ LR(1); LL(1) aur LALR/SLR incomparable (generally), par har LL(1) grammar LR(1) hai.\n- **States**: LR(0) = SLR = LALR (same number); CLR states ≥ LALR.\n- **LALR** = CLR ke same core wale states merge → **R-R conflicts** aa sakte, S-R nahi (agar CLR me nahi the).\n- SLR reduce sirf FOLLOW(A) pe; LR(1) lookahead pe.\n- YACC/Bison → LALR(1).\n\n### Conflicts\n- S-R conflict: ek state me shift aur reduce dono.\n- R-R conflict: do reductions.\n- Ambiguous grammar kisi LR parser se parse nahi (bina precedence rules).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"YACC kaunsa parser?\" → LALR(1).\n2. \"Number of states SLR vs LALR\" → equal.\n3. \"Left recursive grammar LL(1) ho sakta?\" → nahi.\n4. \"Handle pruning\" → bottom-up parsing (rightmost derivation in reverse).","tips":"- Lookahead jitna precise, power utna: LR(0) none, SLR FOLLOW, LALR merged lookahead, LR(1) exact.","sol":"LL(0) → LR(0) → SLR → LALR(1) → LR(1).\n\n**Answer: (C)**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers: Principles, Techniques and Tools (Dragon book), Sec 4.6–4.7 (SLR, canonical LR, LALR)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-89":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Programmed I/O input: **status register padho (A) → flag bit check (B) → data register padho (C) → memory me store (D)**.","concept":"### Programmed I/O (Mano, Fig 11-10)\nCPU loop:\n1. **Read status register** of interface.\n2. **Check flag bit** (data ready?). Flag = 0 → wapas 1 (busy waiting).\n3. Flag = 1 → **read data register** (data CPU me).\n4. **Transfer data to memory**; aur data ho to repeat.\nDrawback: CPU device speed pe atka rehta → interrupt-driven I/O / DMA better.\n\n### Interrupt-driven I/O\nDevice ready → interrupt → CPU ISR me data transfer. **Priority interrupt**: daisy chaining (serial), parallel priority (priority encoder), polling (software).\n\n### Handshaking (asynchronous data transfer)\n- **Strobe** (single control line): source-initiated / destination-initiated.\n- **Handshaking** (2 lines): data valid + data accepted.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Daisy chaining priority\" → device jo CPU ke sabse paas, highest priority.\n2. \"Interrupt vector\" → ISR ka address.\n3. \"Asynchronous serial transfer start/stop bits\" → 1 start, 1–2 stop.","tips":"- Pehle \"ready hai?\" dekho, phir data uthao, phir rakho.","sol":"A → B → C → D.\n\n**Answer: (A) A, B, C, D**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 11.4 (Modes of transfer: programmed I/O flowchart)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-90":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Floating-point add pipeline: **exponents compare (D) → mantissas align (A) → add/subtract (B) → normalize (C)**.","concept":"### Arithmetic pipeline (Mano)\nFloating-point X = A × 2ᵃ, Y = B × 2ᵇ add karne ke 4 segments:\n1. **Compare exponents** (subtraction se difference).\n2. **Align mantissas** (chhote exponent wale mantissa ko right shift).\n3. **Add/subtract mantissas**.\n4. **Normalize** result (leading zeros hatao / overflow pe shift, exponent adjust).\n\n### Pipeline performance\n- k-stage pipeline, n tasks, clock tp: time = (k + n − 1)·tp.\n- Non-pipelined: n·tn (tn = k·tp ideally).\n- **Speedup** S = n·tn / (k + n − 1)·tp → max k (n → ∞).\n- Efficiency = S / k; throughput = n / ((k + n − 1)tp).\n- Unequal stages → clock = max stage delay (+ latch delay).\n\n### Hazards (instruction pipeline)\n- **Structural** (resource conflict), **data** (RAW, WAR, WAW → forwarding/stall), **control** (branch → prediction, delayed branch).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. 4 stages, 100 tasks, tp = 20 ns → (4 + 99)·20 = 2060 ns; non-pipelined tn = 80 → 8000 ns → speedup ≈ 3.88.\n2. Stage delays 150, 120, 160, 140 ns + latch 5 → clock 165 ns.\n3. \"Vector processing\" → SIMD, ek instruction se poore array pe operation.","tips":"- Hand se float add karte waqt bhi yahi steps: exponent dekho, decimal align, jodo, normalize.","sol":"D → A → B → C.\n\n**Answer: (A) D, A, B, C**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 9.4 (Arithmetic pipeline), 9.2 (Pipelining speedup)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-91":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Leaders: (1) pehla statement **100**, (2) jump ke **targets** 105, 111, 107, 103, (3) jump ke **turant baad** wale 104, 105, 106, 107, 111 → {100, 103, 104, 105, 106, 107, 111}.","concept":"### Basic block\nStatements ka maximal sequence jisme control **sirf shuru se ghuse** aur **sirf end se nikle** (beech me koi jump in/out nahi).\n\n### Leaders nikaalne ke rules (Dragon book)\n1. Pehla three-address instruction leader.\n2. Kisi bhi (conditional/unconditional) **jump ka target** leader.\n3. Jump ke **turant baad** wala instruction leader.\nBasic block = leader se agle leader tak (usse pehle tak).\n\n### Flow graph\nNodes = basic blocks; edge B → C agar B ke end se C pe jump ho ya B ke baad C aata ho (aur B unconditional jump pe khatam nahi hota).\n- **Loop**: back edge (target dominates source) se pehchano.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Number of basic blocks = number of leaders (yahan 7).\n2. \"Number of edges in flow graph\" (yahan 8, Q95 dekho).\n3. Matrix multiplication TAC ke leaders — classic Dragon-book example.","tips":"- Teeno lists alag likho (first, targets, after-jumps), phir union karo.","sol":"- First: 100.\n- Targets: 105 (from 103), 111 (from 104, 106), 107 (from 105), 103 (from 110).\n- After jumps: 104 (after 103), 105 (after 104), 106 (after 105), 107 (after 106), 111 (after 110).\nUnion = 100, 103, 104, 105, 106, 107, 111.\nBlocks: B1 100–102, B2 103, B3 104, B4 105, B5 106, B6 107–110, B7 111–112.\n\n**Answer: (D) 100, 103, 104, 105, 106, 107, 111**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon book), Sec 8.4 (Basic blocks and flow graphs: Algorithm 8.5 partitioning into basic blocks)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-92":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Optimizations dekho: **b = y + 2 = t1** (common subexpression), **num = limit − 1 = 9** (constant folding/propagation) aur dono loop-invariant → loop ke bahar; `initial` kabhi use nahi → **dead code** hatao. Ye sab option (C) me hai.","concept":"### Machine-independent code optimizations\n- **Common subexpression elimination (CSE)**: same expression dobara compute na karo (y + 2 already t1 me).\n- **Constant folding**: compile-time pe constant expression evaluate (10 − 1 = 9).\n- **Constant propagation**: variable ki jagah uski known constant value (limit = 10).\n- **Copy propagation**: x = y ke baad x ki jagah y.\n- **Dead code elimination**: jiski value kabhi use nahi hoti (initial).\n- **Loop optimizations**:\n  - **Code motion / loop-invariant hoisting**: loop me jo badalta nahi use loop ke bahar (pre-header).\n  - **Strength reduction**: costly op ko sasta (i × 4 → t = t + 4).\n  - **Induction variable elimination**, loop unrolling, loop jamming (fusion).\n- **Peephole** (machine-dependent): redundant load/store, unreachable code, algebraic simplification (x × 1), jumps over jumps.\n\n### Note on safety\nLoop-invariant ko loop se bahar le jaana tabhi poori tarah safe jab wo loop ke exit point ko dominate kare ya uski purani value baad me use na ho. Exam-level answer (C) yahi optimizations dikhata hai.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. `x = 2 * 3.14 * r` → constant folding: x = 6.28 * r.\n2. `for (i = 0; i < n; i++) a[i] = b * c;` → b * c loop ke bahar.\n3. \"x = x + 0, x = x * 1\" → algebraic simplification (peephole).","tips":"- Option me dhoondo: (1) y + 2 dobara compute ho raha? (2) unused variable bacha? (3) loop me constant assignment?","sol":"(C): t1 = y + 2; limit = 10; num = 9; b = t1 — invariants/CSE/constant bahar; `initial = x/t1` (dead) hata; loop 104–109 me sirf i = i − num. Baaki options me ya dead code bacha hai ya jump targets galat/garbled hain.\n\n**Answer: (C)**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon book), Sec 9.1 (Principal sources of optimization: CSE, copy propagation, dead code, code motion)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-93":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"103: i > j false → exit; 105: num > b false → exit; dono true → body (107–109) → wapas 103. Yani **while (i > j && num > b)**, phir j = i − num, k = j + b.","concept":"### TAC → high-level code (reverse engineering)\n- **Back edge** (goto pehle ke label pe) = **loop**.\n- Do conditions jo dono exit pe jaati → **&&** (dono true hon tabhi body).\n- Agar kisi ek ke true hone pe body chale → **||**.\n- `if cond goto L_body; goto L_exit` pattern = \"if not cond, exit\".\n\n### Short-circuit code\n`while (A && B) S` → \n- L1: if A goto L2; goto Lexit\n- L2: if B goto L3; goto Lexit\n- L3: S; goto L1\n`A || B` → if A goto Lbody; if B goto Lbody; goto Lexit.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. `if (a < b || c > d) x = 1;` ka TAC → 2 conditional jumps to same \"true\" label.\n2. `do { … } while (c)` → body pehle, end me \"if c goto start\".\n3. `for` loop TAC → init, test, body, increment, goto test.","tips":"- Exit dono conditions se → AND. Body dono se → OR.","sol":"Loop: 103 (i > j) aur 105 (num > b) dono true → 107–109 → goto 103. Kisi ek false → 111. Isliye while (i > j && num > b) { num = limit − 1; b = y + 2; i = i − num; } j = i − num; k = j + b.\n(D) yahi structure deta hai (option me last line `k = k+b` print typo hai — TAC me j + b). (A) if (loop nahi), (B) ||, (C) structure galat.\n\n**Answer: (D)**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon book), Sec 6.6 (Control flow: short-circuit code), 6.7 (Backpatching)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-94":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Blocks: B1 100–102, B2 103, B3 104, B4 105, B5 106, B6 107–110, B7 111–112. B7 tak B3 ke bina (B5 se) bhi pahuch sakte → B3 sirf **khud** ko dominate; B5 bhi sirf **khud** ko.","concept":"### Dominators\nNode d **dominates** n (d dom n) agar entry se n tak **har path** d se guzre. Har node khud ko dominate karta. Entry sabko dominate karta.\n- **Immediate dominator**: n ka closest strict dominator → **dominator tree**.\n- **Back edge**: a → b jahan b dom a → natural loop (b header).\n- Natural loop of back edge n → d = d + saare nodes jo d se guzre bina n tak pahunch sakein.\n\n### Is flow graph me\nEdges: B1→B2, B2→B3, B2→B4, B3→B7, B4→B5, B4→B6, B5→B7, B6→B2.\n- B1 dom all. B2 dom B2…B7. B4 dom B4, B5, B6.\n- B7 ke paths: …B2→B3→B7 aur …B4→B5→B7 → B7 ko sirf B1, B2 dominate karte.\n- B6 → B2 back edge (B2 dom B6) → natural loop {B2, B4, B6}.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. \"Which blocks does B4 dominate?\" → B4, B5, B6.\n2. \"Back edge?\" → B6 → B2; loop header B2.\n3. \"Immediate dominator of B7\" → B2.","tips":"- Ek node X, Y ko dominate karta hai tabhi jab X ko graph se hata do to Y unreachable ho jaaye.","sol":"- B3 (104) hata do → B7 phir bhi B5 se reachable → B3 sirf B3 dominate.\n- B5 (106) hata do → B7 B3 se reachable → B5 sirf B5 dominate.\n\n**Answer: (A) Block 3: 3; Block 5: 5**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon book), Sec 9.6 (Loops in flow graphs: dominators, back edges, natural loops)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-95":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"7 blocks → **7 × 7** adjacency matrix. Edges: 1→2, 2→3, 2→4, 3→7, 4→5, 4→6, 5→7, 6→2. Option (A) ki rows exactly yahi hain.","concept":"### Flow graph as adjacency matrix\nRow i, column j = 1 agar block Bi se Bj pe edge.\n- Edge banane ke rules: (1) Bi ka last instruction jump hai to target block pe edge; (2) Bi conditional jump ya normal statement pe khatam ho to agle block (fall-through) pe bhi edge; (3) unconditional goto ke baad fall-through edge **nahi**.\n\n### Is TAC ke blocks/edges\n- B1 (100–102) → B2 (fall-through).\n- B2 (103: if i > j goto 105) → B4 (target), B3 (fall-through).\n- B3 (104: goto 111) → B7.\n- B4 (105: if num > b goto 107) → B6, B5.\n- B5 (106: goto 111) → B7.\n- B6 (107–110, goto 103) → B2.\n- B7 (111–112) → koi nahi (exit).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Number of edges = number of 1s = 8.\n2. \"Exit block\" → jiski row me sab 0.\n3. Cyclomatic complexity = E − N + 2 = 8 − 7 + 2 = 3.","tips":"- Pehle matrix ka size dekho (7 × 7 chahiye) — 6 × 6 wale options (B, C) turant out.","sol":"Rows: B1 0100000, B2 0011000, B3 0000001, B4 0000110, B5 0000001, B6 0100000, B7 0000000 = option (A). (D) ki row 2 (0001010) galat.\n\n**Answer: (A)**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon book), Sec 8.4.3–8.4.4 (Flow graphs, representation)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-96":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"C-LOOK right, head 58: right side ke requests ascending **77, 81, 99, 150, 175, 185**, phir sabse chhote (33, 45) pe jump. 2nd request = **81**.","concept":"### Disk scheduling algorithms\n- **FCFS**: request order me. Fair, par movement zyada.\n- **SSTF** (shortest seek time first): har baar sabse paas wala. Starvation possible.\n- **SCAN (elevator)**: ek direction me **disk ke end tak**, phir ulta.\n- **LOOK**: SCAN jaisa par **last request tak** hi jaata (end tak nahi).\n- **C-SCAN**: ek direction me end tak, phir **doosre end pe jump** (wapsi me service nahi), phir same direction. Uniform wait.\n- **C-LOOK**: C-SCAN jaisa par last request tak, phir **sabse door wale request** (opposite side ka smallest) pe jump.\nHead movement me jump count hota hai ya nahi — question/convention pe depend (Galvin C-SCAN me return count karta).\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. C-LOOK total (jump count karke): (185 − 58) + (185 − 33) + (45 − 33) = 127 + 152 + 12 = 291.\n2. LOOK right total: (185 − 58) + (185 − 33) = 279.\n3. \"Starvation kis me?\" → SSTF.","tips":"- Requests sort karo: 33, 45, | 58 | 77, 81, 99, 150, 175, 185 — har algorithm 10 second ka ho jaata.","sol":"Order: 77 (1st), **81 (2nd)**, 99, 150, 175, 185, → jump → 33, 45.\n\n**Answer: (A) 81**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 11.2 / 10.4 (Disk scheduling: FCFS, SSTF, SCAN, C-SCAN, LOOK, C-LOOK)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-97":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"C-SCAN right (disk 0–199 maan ke): 58 → 199 (**141**), 199 → 0 jump (**199**), 0 → 45 (**45**) → total **385**.","concept":"### C-SCAN calculation pattern\nTotal = (max cylinder − head) + (max − 0) [return jump] + (last request on left side − 0).\n- Disk size question me na diya ho to standard **0–199** (200 cylinders) maana jaata.\n- Agar return jump count na karna ho: (199 − 58) + 45 = 186.\n\n### SCAN vs C-SCAN vs LOOK vs C-LOOK (head 58, right)\n- SCAN: 58 → 199 → 33 = 141 + 166 = 307.\n- C-SCAN: 141 + 199 + 45 = 385.\n- LOOK: 58 → 185 → 33 = 127 + 152 = 279.\n- C-LOOK: 127 + 152 + 12 = 291.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. SCAN right total = 307 (dekho option B — trap).\n2. Disk 0–4999 wale Galvin exercises — same formula.\n3. Seek time per cylinder 1 ms → total time = movement × 1 ms.","tips":"- Options me SCAN (307) aur C-SCAN (385) dono hote — direction aur \"circular\" dhyaan se padho.","sol":"Right: 77, 81, 99, 150, 175, 185 → 199: 199 − 58 = 141.\nJump 199 → 0: 199.\nThen 33, 45: 45 − 0 = 45.\nTotal = 141 + 199 + 45 = **385**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 385**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 10.4 (C-SCAN scheduling)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-98":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"SCAN left, head 58: pehle left side descending — **45 (1st), 33 (2nd)**, phir 0 tak, phir right side.","concept":"### SCAN (elevator)\nHead current direction me chalte hue raste ke saare requests serve karta, disk ke end (0 ya max) tak jaata, phir direction ulat ke baaki serve.\n- Left direction, head 58: 45, 33 → 0 → 77, 81, 99, 150, 175, 185.\n- Total = (58 − 0) + (185 − 0) = 58 + 185 = 243.\n\n### LOOK left: 45, 33, phir 77 … 185 → (58 − 33) + (185 − 33) = 25 + 152 = 177.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. SCAN left total = 243.\n2. C-SCAN left: 58 → 0 (58), 0 → 199 (199), 199 → 77 (122) = 379.\n3. \"Which algorithm is like an elevator?\" → SCAN.","tips":"- \"2nd request\" type me sirf sorted list pe direction me do kadam chalo.","sol":"Left order: 45 (1st), **33 (2nd)**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 33**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 10.4 (SCAN scheduling)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-99":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"SSTF har baar nearest: 58 → 45 (13) → 33 (12) → 77 (44) → 81 (4) → 99 (18) → 150 (51) → 175 (25) → 185 (10) = **177**.","concept":"### SSTF (Shortest Seek Time First)\n- Current head position se sabse paas wala pending request.\n- Greedy; average seek achha par **optimal guarantee nahi**; door wale requests ki **starvation**.\n- Tie ho to question ka convention (usually direction/lower number).\n- SJF scheduling ka disk version.\n\n### Trick\nSorted list bana ke dono taraf ka nearest compare karo; ek side khatam ho jaye to doosri side seedha.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Galvin classic: head 53, queue 98, 183, 37, 122, 14, 124, 65, 67 → SSTF = 236.\n2. Same queue FCFS = 640, SCAN (towards 0) = 236, C-SCAN = 382 (jump count).\n3. \"SSTF optimal hai?\" → nahi.","tips":"- Har step ka distance likho aur end me jodo — calculation errors kam.","sol":"58 → 45: 13; 45 → 33: 12 (33 is 12 away, 77 is 32); 33 → 77: 44; 77 → 81: 4; 81 → 99: 18; 99 → 150: 51; 150 → 175: 25; 175 → 185: 10.\nTotal = 13 + 12 + 44 + 4 + 18 + 51 + 25 + 10 = **177**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 177**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 10.4.2 (SSTF scheduling)."},"ugc-2025-06-p2-100":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"FCFS: queue order me |difference| jodo: 13 + 36 + 104 + 152 + 142 + 76 + 51 + 73 = **647**.","concept":"### FCFS disk scheduling\nRequests exactly aane ke order me serve. Simple, fair, starvation nahi; par wild swings → seek zyada.\n\n### Head movement formula\nTotal = Σ |rᵢ − rᵢ₋₁| (r₀ = initial head).\nAverage seek length = total / number of requests.\n\n### Comparison (is queue, head 58)\nFCFS 647, SSTF 177, SCAN-left 243, SCAN-right 307, C-SCAN-right 385, LOOK-right 279.\n\n### Is topic se aur kya puchha ja sakta hai\n1. Average seek length FCFS = 647 / 8 ≈ 80.9.\n2. Seek time 2 ms per cylinder → 1294 ms.\n3. \"Which gives minimum movement here?\" → SSTF.","tips":"- Running sum likho (49, 153, 305, 447, 523, 574, 647) — check aasaan.","sol":"58 → 45: 13; 45 → 81: 36; 81 → 185: 104; 185 → 33: 152; 33 → 175: 142; 175 → 99: 76; 99 → 150: 51; 150 → 77: 73.\nTotal = **647**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 647**","refs":"- Galvin — Operating System Concepts, Sec 10.4.1 (FCFS scheduling)."}});
